#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""orchestrator — the deterministic wrapper around a judgment core (plan 0004).

Plan 0004's central structural rule: **every guarantee lives here, in code, and
only judgment lives in bin/orchestrator-prompt.md**. A guarantee written as
prompt text is a hope; a drifted prompt cannot masquerade as a control.

WHAT IS WIRED TODAY, AND WHY THAT MATTERS
=========================================
Part 2 is complete. Lease activation, heartbeat, workspace, plan read and
prompt construction (2a); **the implementer session itself** — spawned under a
routed runner, in its own process group, with an allowlisted environment, and
verified afterwards from the forge by `verify_marked_pr` (0010); **the review
loop** under a fixed mandate, with the verdict read back off the forge and bound
to the head SHA it reviewed (0011); **the comment/ack protocol and the steward
phase**, where the pull request thread is the ledger (0031); and **the
post-approval freeze** (0032). `UNBUILT_PHASES` is empty and `--ready` exits 0,
which is what lets `fleet-watch` arm.

The session is the judgment core; everything around it here is a guarantee. In
particular a zero exit from the session proves nothing: the run succeeds only
when a PR authored by an implementer account, on this lease's branch, based on
main, carrying `Plan: <id>`, with the plan flipped to `done`, is readable from
the forge. A model that reports success having opened nothing must fail, because
dedupe is "a marked PR exists" — an unmarked PR means the plan re-dispatches
while its work sits in a branch nobody will look at.

That gap is dangerous in a specific way, so it is handled explicitly rather than
left to fall out. Until this file existed, `fleet-watch` spawning an
orchestrator raised OSError, and the watcher's handler released the lease,
emitted `plan-failed`, and paged. The moment a file exists at this path the
spawn SUCCEEDS, the watcher emits `plan-dispatched`, and the plan reads as
in-flight — so a half-built wrapper that merely exited would convert a loud
failure into a plan that looks armed and is not. Ignition must never look
armed when it is not.

While phases were unbuilt, the boundary between built and unbuilt was a real,
loud stop (`_unbuilt()`), and each merged PR moved it later without ever making
it silent. Nothing is unbuilt now: the boundary does not move again and that
helper is gone rather than left as a page reason no caller can produce.

What survives from it is the rule it enforced, which `_stop` still carries: a
failure does **not** release the lease. Releasing would return the plan to the
undispatched pool and the watcher would re-dispatch it every cycle; holding it
lets the lease lapse into orphanhood, the fleet's surface-once-never-respawn
path. The one legitimate release is the steward's, when the marked PR is merged
or closed.

NO APPROVAL CODE PATH
=====================
There is no function here that posts a Forgejo review, and there must never be.
The orchestrator is graded on "revbot approved," so the credential
separation is not enough on its own — the absence of the code path is the
control (plan 0004, tested by asserting zero approval calls across the suite).

The boundary is also declared UPWARD, which is the half that keeps the fleet
quiet. `fleet-watch` already refuses to arm when no orchestrator is executable
(its r5 M2 gate), precisely so a part-built deployment does not page once per
plan per cycle. Merely existing at this path would satisfy `os.access` and
defeat that gate — the storm would return through a different door. So this
file answers `--ready` with a non-zero exit until the loop is whole, and the
watcher asks before it arms.

Usage (the contract fleet-watch spawns against):
  orchestrator <owner/repo> <plan-path> <lease-id>
  orchestrator --ready          # exit 0 only when the loop can carry a plan

Env: EUNOMIA_SESSION (holder identity, set by the watcher), EUNOMIA_PLAN_ID,
EUNOMIA_PLAN_ZONE, EUNOMIA_PLAN_TIER, FLEET_WORK_ROOT, FLEET_NTFY_URL,
FLEET_HEARTBEAT_SECS (default 60), EUNOMIA_INITIATOR (who filed this run;
unset means the operator), EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER (per-run runner override — sits
above the repo's own choice; unset, the implementer runner comes from
`bin/fleet-models`'s repo_model table via resolved_implementer_env, plan 0048),
EUNOMIA_TRANSCRIPT_DIR (override for the transcript root),
FLEET_IMPLEMENTER_UIDS / FLEET_IMPLEMENTER_LOGINS (who may author a marked PR),
FLEET_FORGEJO_URL.

The implementer's wall-clock bound is per repo, read from
config/impl-bounds.conf (plan 0042) — there is no environment override, and a
missing or malformed conf is a Stop, not a quiet 90.

Two paths a run writes, both named on every page it sends:
  <work root>/logs/<lease-id>.log        structured facts, redacted
  ~/dev/.orchestrator-transcripts/<lease-id>.log   the session's own output,
                                         redacted, 0600, deliberately OUTSIDE
                                         the fleet tree (SPEC Principle 6).
"""
import base64
import collections
import contextlib
import json
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import os
import re
import resource
import shlex
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

BIN = Path(__file__).resolve().parent

_lib_loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("fleetlib", str(BIN / "fleetlib.py"))
_lib_spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("fleetlib", _lib_loader)
lib = importlib.util.module_from_spec(_lib_spec)
_lib_loader.exec_module(lib)


def _load_sibling(name, mod_name):
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(mod_name, str(BIN / name))
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(mod_name, loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


def warn(msg):
    print(f"orchestrator: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


class Stop(Exception):
    """A refusal that already knows what the page should say.

    Every routing, credential, harness and transcript refusal raises this rather
    than returning a sentinel, so `main` has exactly one place that turns a
    refusal into `_stop` — and so a new refusal cannot be added that forgets to
    page. `reason` is the whole message: the watcher spawns with stderr=DEVNULL,
    so the page's reason is the only channel that survives."""

    def __init__(self, reason):
        super().__init__(reason)
        self.reason = reason


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ secrecy
def _redaction_patterns():
    """The HIGH-SIGNAL shapes from fleet-leak-watch, and deliberately not its
    PARANOID set.

    Reused rather than restated so one list governs both the monitor and the
    writer; a second copy would drift and the drift would be invisible until a
    leak. `PARANOID_FINDINGS` is excluded on purpose: it matches `long-hex`,
    which is every git SHA — and a head SHA is the single most load-bearing
    fact an approval binds to (§4.6). Redacting it to be safe would corrupt the
    evidence this thread exists to carry."""
    lw = _load_sibling("fleet-leak-watch", "_leak_watch")
    return list(lw.FINDINGS)


def redact(text):
    """Everything written to a PR thread passes through here (SPEC Principle 6).

    Returns (clean_text, [shape names found]). The caller is expected to treat a
    non-empty finding list as a fact worth surfacing, not merely as a
    substitution that quietly happened."""
    found = []
    out = text or ""
    for name, rx in _redaction_patterns():
        if rx.search(out):
            found.append(name)
            out = rx.sub(f"[redacted:{name}]", out)
    return out, found


def _transcript_patterns():
    """FINDINGS **+ PARANOID_FINDINGS**, which is not what `redact()` uses.

    The difference is the whole point, so it is stated rather than assumed. The
    credential an implementer session actually holds on this fleet is a Forgejo
    PAT: 40 hex characters, no prefix, no structure. Not one shape in FINDINGS
    matches that — `long-hex` is the only pattern that does, and it lives in the
    PARANOID set. A transcript redacted with FINDINGS alone would pass every
    "we redact transcripts" review and still carry the token.

    `redact()` keeps the narrow set, and correctly: it governs PR comments,
    where `long-hex` is every git SHA and a head SHA is the single most
    load-bearing fact an approval binds to (SPEC §4.6). A transcript has no such
    field to protect — it is free text — so the noisy shapes cost nothing here
    and the SHA objection does not transfer."""
    lw = _load_sibling("fleet-leak-watch", "_leak_watch")
    return list(lw.FINDINGS) + list(lw.PARANOID_FINDINGS)


def redact_transcript(text):
    """Redact the BUFFERED WHOLE. Never call this per read or per line.

    A 40-hex token that lands with 12 characters at the end of one `read()` and
    28 at the start of the next matches no per-chunk regex — each half is short,
    both halves are printable, and the writer that stitched them back together
    would have written the token out. The reader thread therefore accumulates
    chunks and nothing is substituted until the process is done and the buffer
    is joined."""
    found = []
    out = text or ""
    for name, rx in _transcript_patterns():
        if rx.search(out):
            found.append(name)
            out = rx.sub(f"[redacted:{name}]", out)
    return out, found


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the log
def check_evidence_paths(lease_id):
    """Resolve both evidence paths BEFORE the session is spawned.

    `transcript_dir()` used to be first called by `write_transcript`, AFTER the
    session — so a transcript root inside the fleet tree failed a 90-minute run
    at the very end, and `main` reported it as "the session failed to run" for a
    session that had run perfectly.

    Worse, that path skipped `verify_marked_pr` entirely: if the session HAD
    opened a marked PR, dedupe would treat the plan as done on a pull request
    nothing verified, while the page said the session never ran (review 2369
    M3). A misconfiguration must fail before the spend, not after it."""
    lease_key(lease_id)
    return transcript_dir()


def set_commit_identity(worktree):
    """Configure who this worktree commits as, BEFORE the session runs.

    From the live scratch-repo run (review of #223): the session committed as
    `Fleet Operator <operator@opshost.example>` — the operator — so git history
    said a human wrote model-generated code while every check passed, because
    `verify_marked_pr` reads the PULL REQUEST's author and never the commit's.
    Three such commits are on main today.

    In code rather than in the implementer prompt, because a guarantee written
    as prompt text is a hope: the session can forget it, paraphrase it, or be
    handed a `-c user.name` by something else. Local to the worktree, so it
    cannot alter the operator's global config.

    Not fatal if it fails. A run that cannot set an identity still produces work
    a human reviews; refusing here would trade a wrong `Author:` line for no
    plan at all."""
    who = lib.agent_roles()["author"].commit_identity
    name, _, email = who.partition("<")
    name, email = name.strip(), email.rstrip(">").strip()
    if not name or not email:
        warn(f"FLEET_COMMIT_IDENTITY={who!r} is not 'Name <email>' — leaving the "
             "worktree's identity alone")
        return False
    for key, value in (("user.name", name), ("user.email", email)):
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "config", "--local", key, value],
                           cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=60)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            warn(f"could not set {key} on {worktree}: {r.stderr.strip()[:120]}")
            return False
    return True


def lease_key(lease_id):
    """The lease id, refused unless it is id-shaped.

    Every file this module names is `<somewhere>/<lease id>...`, so the check
    belongs at the ONE place that turns an id into a path rather than at the
    last of them. `write_transcript` validated and `RunLog` did not — and
    `RunLog` opens FIRST, so `../../x` was appended under the work root before
    anything rejected it (review 2369 L3)."""
    if not lib._ID_RE.match(lease_id or ""):
        raise Stop(f"{lease_id!r} is not a lease id — refusing to name a file with it")
    return lease_id


def run_log_path(lease_id):
    """`<work root>/logs/<lease-id>.log` — inside the fleet tree on purpose.

    Principle 6 forbids secret material under this tree, not writing under it.
    This file carries structured facts only — phase, exit code, fault class,
    paths — and every line still passes through `redact_transcript` before it is
    written, because "structured facts only" is a discipline and the redactor is
    a control."""
    return lib.work_root() / "logs" / f"{lease_id}.log"


class RunLog:
    """Opened BEFORE the lease is activated, and never a failure channel.

    Before, because the watcher spawns this process with `stderr=DEVNULL`
    (`bin/fleet-watch:791`): a failure between spawn and the first reachable
    forge call currently leaves nothing at all to read. A log opened after the
    step that failed documents every run except the ones worth documenting.

    Never a failure channel, because the page is: a failure that only appends to
    a log is a failure nobody sees. What the log buys is that the page's one-line
    reason has somewhere to point, which is why every page names this path.

    Its own open failure is warned about and swallowed — a full disk must not
    take down a run that could still open a PR."""

    def __init__(self, lease_id):
        # Validated HERE, not later: this is the first thing in the run that
        # turns the argv lease id into a path, and it opens the file.
        self.path = run_log_path(lease_key(lease_id))
        self._fh = None
        try:
            self.path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            self._fh = open(self.path, "a", encoding="utf-8")
        except OSError as e:
            warn(f"run log {self.path} unavailable ({e.__class__.__name__})")

    def note(self, phase, **facts):
        parts = [lib.ts(lib.now_utc()), phase]
        parts += [f"{k}={v}" for k, v in facts.items()]
        clean, _ = redact_transcript(" ".join(str(x) for x in parts))
        if self._fh is None:
            return
        try:
            self._fh.write(clean + "\n")
            self._fh.flush()
        except OSError:
            pass

    def close(self):
        try:
            if self._fh is not None:
                self._fh.close()
        except OSError:
            pass


# ------------------------------------------------------------ the transcript
def transcript_dir():
    """`~/dev/.orchestrator-transcripts`, and provably not under `~/dev/.fleet`.

    SPEC Principle 6: nothing under the fleet tree may contain secret material,
    and atlas renders that tree. A session transcript is the one artefact here
    most likely to contain some — so it lives outside, 0600, and only its PATH
    is ever quoted into an event, a page or (from 0011) a PR comment.

    The containment check is code rather than a convention because
    `EUNOMIA_TRANSCRIPT_DIR` exists for the suite, and a test fixture pointing
    it inside the fleet tree would otherwise silently reintroduce exactly what
    this function exists to prevent."""
    env = (os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_TRANSCRIPT_DIR") or "").strip()
    d = Path(env).expanduser() if env else Path.home() / "dev" / ".orchestrator-transcripts"
    d = d.expanduser().resolve()
    fleet = lib.fleet_dir().expanduser().resolve()
    if d == fleet or str(d).startswith(str(fleet) + os.sep):
        raise Stop(f"transcript dir {d} is inside the fleet tree {fleet} — "
                   "SPEC Principle 6 forbids session output there")
    return d


# A TAG is not a lease id. An earlier revision validated tags against
# `lib._ID_RE`, which is `^[a-z]+--slug--\d{3,}$` — the lease-id shape — so NO
# tag this file generates (`r1`, `c10`, `f10r1`, `u10r2`) could ever match it,
# and every fix round, steward instruction and follow-up session raised Stop
# AFTER doing its work and was reported as refused. The whole of 0011/0031/0032
# was broken at runtime and the suite did not see it, because the tests that
# exercise those paths stub `run_implementer`. Found as a HIGH in review 2422.
# This pattern admits what the code produces and still cannot traverse: no `/`,
# no `.`, so no `..`.
_TAG_RE = re.compile(r"\A[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*\Z")


def write_transcript(lease_id, text, tag=None):
    """The session's whole output, redacted as one buffer, mode 0600.

    Returns (path, shapes found). A non-empty shape list is a fact worth
    surfacing — the token helper's own header records the 2026-08-28 leak where
    a PAT reached a transcript — not merely a substitution that quietly
    happened."""
    d = transcript_dir()
    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    try:
        os.chmod(d, 0o700)
    except OSError:
        pass
    lease_id = lease_key(lease_id)   # one validation point; see lease_key
    # A `tag` gives each session in a run its OWN file. Without it every
    # steward instruction and every fix round reopened one path with O_TRUNC and
    # destroyed the previous session's transcript — the artefact a failure page
    # points the reader at.
    if tag is not None and not _TAG_RE.match(tag):
        raise Stop(f"{tag!r} is not tag-shaped — refusing to name a file with it")
    path = d / (f"{lease_id}-{tag}.log" if tag else f"{lease_id}.log")
    clean, found = redact_transcript(text)
    fd = os.open(str(path), os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(clean)
    os.chmod(str(path), 0o600)
    return path, found


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ lifecycle
def activate(lease_id):
    """Verification, not acquisition: fleet-claim refuses unless EUNOMIA_SESSION
    matches the holder the watcher named. A wrapper that ran without its lease
    would push on a branch row 6's pre-push hook has granted to someone else."""
    r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(BIN / "fleet-claim"), "--activate", lease_id],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    return r.returncode == 0, (r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:200]


# No release() here on purpose. Part 2a has no path that legitimately retires a
# lease: every exit it can reach is a failure with no marked PR, or a VERIFIED
# marked PR that is not merged yet — and docs/plan-dispatch.md forbids releasing
# without a merged one. The steward phase — which exits when the marked PR is
# merged or closed — is where a release belongs, and it should arrive with that
# caller rather than wait here as a function anything could reach.


class Heartbeat:
    """A daemon thread touching the session heartbeat.

    Daemon on purpose: if the main thread dies, the heartbeat must die with it.
    A surviving heartbeat would keep a dead run looking alive, and `is_orphaned`
    — which the watcher's death detection rests on — derives from exactly this
    mtime."""

    def __init__(self, sid, secs=None):
        self.sid = sid
        # not a bare int(): this runs AFTER the lease is activated, so a
        # malformed knob would raise here and strand a live lease with no
        # heartbeat — the one state the heartbeat exists to make detectable.
        self.secs = int(secs) if secs else lib.heartbeat_secs()
        self._stop = threading.Event()
        self._t = None

    def start(self):
        lib.touch_heartbeat(self.sid)
        self._t = threading.Thread(target=self._run, daemon=True)
        self._t.start()
        return self

    def _run(self):
        while not self._stop.wait(self.secs):
            lib.touch_heartbeat(self.sid)

    def stop(self):
        self._stop.set()


# The page's durable half. Every page is written here whether or not it was
# delivered, because the watcher spawns with stderr=DEVNULL and an undelivered
# page currently vanishes without trace — which is how this fleet ran for weeks
# with FLEET_NTFY_URL empty on every unit and nobody noticing.
#
# A flat append-only file under the ledger tree, per SPEC Principle 2, in the
# directory lynceus already reads. NOT fleet.db: that is fleet-collect's, and a
# second writer to it would need its own decision.
PAGES = "pages.jsonl"


def pages_path():
    return lib.fleet_dir() / PAGES


def record_page(title, body, delivered, detail="", extra=None):
    """Append one page to the log. Never raises — a logging failure must not
    take down the thing it is logging, and the page has already been attempted
    by the time we get here."""
    row = {"ts": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()),
           "session": os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_SESSION", ""),
           "title": title, "body": body,
           "delivered": bool(delivered), "detail": detail}
    row.update(extra or {})
    try:
        path = pages_path()
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        clean, found = redact(json.dumps(row, ensure_ascii=False))
        if found:
            warn(f"redacted {sorted(found)} from a page record")
        with open(path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(clean + "\n")
    except Exception as e:
        warn(f"could not record the page ({e.__class__.__name__})")


def angelia_token():
    """The per-app service token, from a COMMAND, never a plist value.

    `FLEET_ANGELIA_TOKEN_CMD` runs and prints the token; the value lives in one
    local and one header and is never logged. Absent means the angelia channel
    is not configured, which is a state to report rather than an error."""
    cmd = os.environ.get("FLEET_ANGELIA_TOKEN_CMD", "").strip()
    if not cmd:
        return None
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(shlex.split(cmd), capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        warn(f"angelia token command failed ({e.__class__.__name__})")
        return None
    lines = [l.strip() for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()]
    if r.returncode != 0 or not lines:
        warn("angelia token command produced no token")
        return None
    return lines[-1]


def send_angelia(title, body, timeout=10):
    """(delivered, detail). Delivery is judged from angelia's COUNTS.

    A 200 with `sent: 0` is not a page — the device registry can be empty for
    this app and user, and the request still succeeds. Reading the status code
    alone is how a channel comes to be believed while silent, which is the
    failure this whole change exists to end."""
    base = os.environ.get("FLEET_ANGELIA_URL", "").strip().rstrip("/")
    app = os.environ.get("FLEET_ANGELIA_APP", "").strip()
    user = os.environ.get("FLEET_ANGELIA_USER", "operator").strip()
    if not base or not app:
        return False, "angelia not configured (FLEET_ANGELIA_URL/APP unset)"
    token = angelia_token()
    if not token:
        return False, "angelia token unavailable"
    payload = json.dumps({"user": user,
                          "payload": {"title": title, "body": body}}).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        f"{base}/api/v1/send", data=payload, method="POST",
        headers={"Content-Type": "application/json",
                 "X-Angelia-App": app, "X-Angelia-Token": token})
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=timeout) as resp:
            raw = resp.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")
            code = resp.status
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        # The body can echo the request; never keep it. 404 is the one worth
        # naming, because it means "authenticated fine, nobody is listening".
        detail = ("no device registered for this app and user"
                  if e.code == 404 else f"HTTP {e.code}")
        return False, detail
    except Exception as e:
        return False, f"{e.__class__.__name__}"
    try:
        counts = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        return False, f"HTTP {code} with an unreadable body"
    sent = counts.get("sent")
    if not isinstance(sent, int) or sent < 1:
        return False, (f"HTTP {code} but sent={sent!r} — accepted and delivered "
                       "to nobody")
    return True, f"sent={sent}"


def notify(title, body):
    """Page a human, and RECORD the attempt either way.

    Two channels, tried in order and both optional: angelia (headers + JSON,
    the fleet's real push path) and FLEET_NTFY_URL (a bare text POST, ntfy's
    contract and no one else's — pointing it at angelia yields a 401 the caller
    swallows, which is why it is not reused here).

    Returns whether the page reached anyone. The log entry is written whatever
    the answer, and that is the point: an undelivered page must leave a trace."""
    delivered, detail = False, ""
    if os.environ.get("FLEET_ANGELIA_URL", "").strip():
        delivered, detail = send_angelia(title, body)

    url = os.environ.get("FLEET_NTFY_URL", "")
    if not delivered and url:
        try:
            req = urllib.request.Request(url, data=f"{title}: {body}".encode(),
                                         method="POST")
            urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=5).read()
            delivered, detail = True, "ntfy"
        except Exception as e:
            detail = f"{detail}; ntfy {e.__class__.__name__}".strip("; ")

    if not delivered:
        # stderr is DEVNULL under the watcher, so this line is for a human
        # running it by hand. The log entry below is the one that survives.
        print(f"orchestrator: NOTIFY(undelivered) {title} — {body}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        if not detail:
            detail = "no channel configured"
        warn(f"page not delivered: {detail}")
    record_page(title, body, delivered, detail)
    return delivered


def emit(event_type, repo, detail, pr=None):
    """Awareness, not authority — a failed emit never gates a transition.
    lease=None always: SPEC requires a null lease on plan-* events, so the
    lease id rides in detail (the rule fleet-watch's emit documents)."""
    try:
        mod = _load_sibling("fleet-emit", "_fleet_emit")
        mod.FLEET_DIR = lib.fleet_dir()
        mod.EVENTS = lib.fleet_dir() / "events.jsonl"
        mod.LOCK = lib.fleet_dir() / "locks" / "events.lock"
        mod.emit(event_type, os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_SESSION", "orchestrator"),
                 repo=repo, pr=pr, lease=None, detail=detail)
    except (Exception, SystemExit) as e:
        warn(f"emit {event_type} failed ({e.__class__.__name__}) — continuing")


# ----------------------------------------------------------- the credential
Credential = collections.namedtuple("Credential", "kind initiator note")

# initiator -> credential kind. Deliberately tiny, and deliberately not a
# fallback table: today the fleet has exactly one credential, and the seam
# exists so that adding a second is a row here rather than a rewrite.
#
# Branches that do NOT exist yet, and what each needs before it can:
#   * a Forgejo person (a PR merger, per plan 0015) -> needs a uid->principal
#     map, so a run filed by someone else bills someone else.
#   * `api_key` -> needs an API key path (a keyvault pull at startup, not an
#     inherited variable) before any runner declaring `needs="api_key"` can run.
_INITIATOR_CREDENTIALS = {
    "operator": "local_cli",
}


def resolve_credential(initiator=None):
    """Which credential this run may spend, from who filed it.

    **Unset means the operator** — the normal production path, and today's only
    credential: `local_cli`, the operator's own `claude` CLI with no token in the
    environment, on whichever host runs `fleet-watch`. `docs/plan-dispatch.md`
    fixed that as opshost, as the logged-in user, on 2026-09-01; plan 0004's
    Resources section still says cihost and is superseded — the session runs
    where the watcher spawns it. The watcher learning the real initiator from
    the plan PR's merger is 0015's work, in `bin/fleet-watch`'s lane, not here.

    A value that is **set and unrecognised** stops the run loudly. That branch is
    reachable in production by setting the variable — not only by a test double
    — which is the point: falling back to the operator would be the plausible
    kindness and is wrong, because a run filed by someone else must bill someone
    else."""
    if initiator is None:
        initiator = os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_INITIATOR", "")
    initiator = (initiator or "").strip()
    if not initiator:
        return Credential("local_cli", "operator",
                          "EUNOMIA_INITIATOR unset — the operator's own CLI on this host")
    kind = _INITIATOR_CREDENTIALS.get(initiator)
    if kind is None:
        raise Stop(f"EUNOMIA_INITIATOR={initiator!r} names no known principal — "
                   f"known: {sorted(_INITIATOR_CREDENTIALS)}. Refusing to fall back "
                   "to the operator's credential: a run filed by someone else "
                   "must bill someone else")
    return Credential(kind, initiator, f"initiator {initiator} -> {kind}")


# -------------------------------------------------------------- the runners
#
# `family` is what a policy may test a runner by — never its name. The reviewer
# positions accept `family == "fable"` only (plan 0065, bin/fleet-models), and a
# substring test on the name breaks the day a runner is called `api-fable`, or
# admits one called `sub-sonnet-fable-ish`.
#
# `settings_must_not_grant` is a tuple of tilde-paths to a vendor's OWN settings
# file, checked by `check_settings_precondition` before that runner is ever
# spawned (plan 0066 D4b). Empty for every runner but `sub-antigravity`: it is
# the one vendor CLI with no flag that ignores its own config, so the control is
# a precondition on the file rather than an argv entry. Defaulted to `()` so
# every existing call site that builds a `Runner` positionally keeps working.
#
# The path is `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json`, NOT
# `~/.gemini/settings.json` — an earlier revision pinned the latter and the
# precondition passed against every real grant because antigravity never reads
# that file. Confirmed 2026-09-22 against the installed 1.2.8 binary's own
# embedded docs: "The CLI is configured via `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/
# settings.json`." (`strings /opt/homebrew/bin/antigravity`, "## 3.
# Configuration" section). `~/.gemini/settings.json` belongs to the legacy
# `gemini` CLI, a different binary (see D3/Boundaries on why it is not added
# here).
#
# NOT covered: the same embedded changelog also documents "project-specific
# configurations (located in `~/.gemini/config/projects/`) take precedence
# over global settings" — a second, cwd-dependent grant surface. Which project
# file that is depends on the tester's cwd, and there is no tester spawn
# constructor yet (see the `--add-dir` deferral in this plan's Boundaries) —
# so it cannot be named here and is left for whoever builds that spawn path.
Runner = collections.namedtuple(
    "Runner", "name argv local needs extra_env unwired family "
              "settings_must_not_grant",
    defaults=((),))

# The permission mode and tool allowlist are NAMED, and what they are worth is
# named with them. `--allowedTools` is surface reduction — no WebFetch, no
# WebSearch, no Task — and nothing more: Claude Code matches Bash permissions on
# the command PREFIX and never inspects git's arguments, so even a
# `Bash(git:*)`-shaped entry admits `git -c alias.x='!<anything>' x`,
# `git -c core.sshCommand=<anything> fetch` and `git -C /any/path`. That is
# arbitrary execution as the operator's user, and a starting directory confines
# nothing either. What actually bounds the damage is stated where it lives:
# verify_marked_pr afterwards, branch protection upstream, and _ENV_ALLOW below.
# `--dangerously-skip-permissions` is never passed, and a test asserts the
# string appears nowhere in this file.
PERMISSION_MODE = "acceptEdits"
TOOL_ALLOWLIST = ("Bash", "Read", "Write", "Edit", "Glob", "Grep", "TodoWrite")
# `--output-format stream-json` (which REQUIRES `--verbose` under `-p`, and the
# CLI refuses without it) because the default text format is the wrong stream to
# redact.
#
# Under `-p`, text format prints the final assistant message and nothing else.
# Tool results — which is where a credential actually lands, as the 2026-08-28
# PAT did — never reach this wrapper's pipe at all, so `redact_transcript` has
# been running over a stream that structurally cannot contain the thing it
# exists to remove.
#
# Measured 2026-09-10 as a controlled A/B: same prompt, same tool call, one file
# whose contents appear ONLY in tool output.
#
#     text format          5 bytes    tool output captured: NO
#     stream-json     15,556 bytes    tool output captured: YES
#
# Record types in the stream-json run: system 11, assistant 4, user 1, result 1.
# The `user` records carry the tool_result blocks; that is the whole difference.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLOSE, and the docs say so rather than implying otherwise:
# Claude Code writes its own full session — tool_use and tool_result included —
# to ~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<uuid>.jsonl, unredacted, whatever this flag says.
# That file is a third path to the same material and it still exists; HOME stays
# on the implementer allowlist, so the session can read it. This change makes the
# transcript the fleet KEEPS honest. It does not make the fleet's exposure zero.
#
# It also stops the fleet being unable to debug itself. Twice on 2026-09-10 a run
# had to be diagnosed by reading Claude Code's own JSONL, because the transcript
# the orchestrator wrote was the model's closing summary and carried no evidence
# of what it had actually run.
_CLAUDE = ("claude", "-p",
           "--output-format", "stream-json", "--verbose",
           "--permission-mode", PERMISSION_MODE,
           "--allowedTools", ",".join(TOOL_ALLOWLIST))

# Absolute, not resolved from PATH at import time (plan 0066 D1). launchd
# spawns this wrapper with PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin — no
# /opt/homebrew/bin — so a lookup made from *this* process's own PATH would
# still find nothing at dispatch time even if it succeeded on whatever host
# wrote the code. This fleet already has the failure on record with a bare
# name: "runner 'sub-sonnet' needs 'claude', which is not on PATH on this
# host — refusing to spawn". Measured 2026-09-21/22 on opshost.
ANTIGRAVITY_BIN = "/opt/homebrew/bin/antigravity"
CODEX_BIN = "/opt/homebrew/bin/codex"

# antigravity's only stdin path (`--input-format stream-json`) takes one line
# of this exact shape, measured against a real request 2026-09-22: any other
# event is ignored with a warning, and a `user` event with no `message` is an
# error (plan 0066 D2).
def antigravity_stdin_line(prompt):
    return json.dumps({"event": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": prompt}})


# antigravity's reply, under `--output-format stream-json`, is the line whose
# `event` is `result` — not plain text, and not the only line: the CLI writes
# warnings to the same stream, interleaved and not JSON, so a reader that
# assumes every line parses breaks on the first one (plan 0066 D2). Returns the
# parsed `result` record, or None if the stream carries none.
def read_antigravity_result(text):
    for line in (text or "").splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            rec = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("event") == "result":
            return rec
    return None


def check_settings_precondition(runner):
    """Refuse `runner` before it is spawned if any file in its
    `settings_must_not_grant` carries a `permissions.allow` entry or a `hooks`
    block — the two ways an operator setting could widen a headless vendor run
    (plan 0066 D4b). A missing or unreadable file grants nothing and passes.

    Not called from any production spawn path today: there is no tester spawn
    constructor yet (plan 0066 Boundaries — nothing dispatches a test lane).
    This is the precondition itself, exercised directly by
    tests/test_vendor_runners.py and by the plan's manual check, ready for
    whichever spawn path calls it first."""
    for rel in runner.settings_must_not_grant:
        path = Path(rel).expanduser()
        try:
            data = json.loads(path.read_text())
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        allow = (data.get("permissions") or {}).get("allow")
        if allow:
            raise Stop(f"{path} grants permissions.allow={allow!r} — refusing to "
                       f"spawn {runner.name!r} headless with a setting that could "
                       "widen it")
        if "hooks" in data:
            raise Stop(f"{path} carries a hooks block — refusing to spawn "
                       f"{runner.name!r} headless with a setting that could "
                       "widen it")
    return True


# A registry of RUNNERS, not a model name. Each entry declares the harness to
# spawn, whether it is local, and which credential kind it requires. A runner
# whose required kind the resolved credential does not provide is a hard stop,
# never a fallback: an `api-sonnet` run served by `local_cli` silently spends the
# operator's subscription while every event and cost report calls it metered.
RUNNERS = {
    "sub-sonnet": Runner("sub-sonnet", _CLAUDE + ("--model", "sonnet"),
                         False, "local_cli", (), "", "claude"),
    "sub-opus": Runner("sub-opus", _CLAUDE + ("--model", "opus"),
                       False, "local_cli", (), "", "claude"),
    # Pinned to the exact id, never an alias: `--model fable` would let the
    # reviewer's model move without a decision. `claude-fable-5.1` and
    # `claude-fable-51` are both rejected by the catalog — the id is
    # `claude-fable-5-1`, as `MODEL` in ~/agent/dispatch-review.py (operator/talos)
    # carries it. This is the runner the reviewer positions accept (plan 0065).
    "sub-fable": Runner("sub-fable", _CLAUDE + ("--model", "claude-fable-5-1"),
                        False, "local_cli", (), "", "fable"),
    "api-sonnet": Runner("api-sonnet", _CLAUDE + ("--model", "sonnet"),
                         False, "api_key", ("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY",), "", "claude"),
    # Declared, and honestly not wired. The agent-bus worker takes a task file
    # off the bus and polls; it is not a command this wrapper can hand a prompt
    # on stdin. Naming a path that exists but cannot accept a prompt would be
    # worse than naming none — the spawn would succeed and do something else —
    # so `unwired` carries the reason and reaching this runner is a hard stop
    # with its own message. It is the only runner a `zone: private` plan can
    # resolve to, so that path fails loudly and legibly rather than silently.
    "local-qwen": Runner("local-qwen", (), True, None, ("OLLAMA_HOST",),
                         "the agent-bus worker harness consumes a task file from "
                         "the bus, not a prompt on stdin — see plan 0010 §5",
                         "qwen"),
    # A test lane, not an implementer (plan 0066). `local=False`: both spend a
    # cloud backend, so ADR-0006 §7's zone allowlist gates them exactly as it
    # gates `sub-sonnet`. `needs="local_cli"`: like the Claude runners, they
    # spend the operator's own login on this host (`~/.gemini/`, `~/.codex/`),
    # never a token in the environment.
    #
    # argv is a PINNED LITERAL TUPLE, asserted by equality in
    # tests/test_vendor_runners.py — not a prefix or a subset check — so no
    # flag outside it can be present (D4b). The `<schema>` and `<out>` tokens
    # are filled in by whatever spawns a tester, never by configuration; no
    # such spawn path exists yet (see `unwired` below and the plan's
    # Boundaries), so they stay literal placeholders here.
    "sub-antigravity": Runner(
        "sub-antigravity",
        (ANTIGRAVITY_BIN, "--print=", "--input-format", "stream-json",
         "--output-format", "stream-json", "--mode", "plan", "--sandbox",
         "--json-schema", "<schema>"),
        False, "local_cli", (), "", "google",
        ("~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/settings.json",)),
    # `unwired`, deliberately, and for a DIFFERENT reason than `local-qwen`'s:
    # the harness can take a prompt on stdin, but `-s read-only` confines only
    # writes (ADR-0006 §6) — reads are confined by the workspace, not the CLI,
    # and this plan does not build that isolation. Reaching it is a hard stop
    # with its own message, same as `local-qwen`.
    "sub-codex": Runner(
        "sub-codex",
        (CODEX_BIN, "exec", "-s", "read-only", "--skip-git-repo-check",
         "--ignore-user-config", "--output-schema", "<schema>", "-o", "<out>"),
        False, "local_cli", (),
        "codex exec -s read-only confines writes, not reads (ADR-0006 §6) — "
        "isolation outside the CLI (a container, a VM, or a home holding only "
        "the bundle) does not exist yet, so this runner cannot be spawned "
        "blind. See plan 0066 Boundaries",
        "openai"),
}
DEFAULT_RUNNER = "sub-sonnet"


def resolved_implementer_env(repo, env=None):
    """`env`, with `EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER` filled in from `repo_model` when the
    operator did not already set it (plan 0048 D6).

    This is where `DEFAULT_RUNNER` stopped being the first choice for a real
    dispatch: `main()` calls this before `route_implementer`, so by the time
    that function reads `EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER` it is either the operator's own
    override or the name `bin/fleet-models`'s `repo_model` table resolved for
    this repo — never a hardcoded constant. A repo with no row, and no
    `'default'` row either, is a `Stop`: plan 0048 D3 is explicit that a
    setting silently reverting to a code default is an unattended process
    spending more than the operator set, and a model doing the same is the
    same defect, just harder to notice.

    Returns a NEW mapping; the caller's `env` (or `os.environ`) is never
    mutated, so a route computed for one repo cannot leak into another."""
    base = dict(os.environ if env is None else env)
    if (base.get("EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER") or "").strip():
        return base
    models = _load_sibling("fleet-models", "_fleet_models")
    try:
        name, _layer = models.resolve_repo(repo, "implementer")
    except models.NotConfigured:
        # repo_model has never been created on this install: nothing has
        # adopted plan 0048 yet, so route_implementer's own DEFAULT_RUNNER
        # fallback applies exactly as it always has. The first `fleet-models`
        # write anywhere creates the table, and this branch stops being
        # reachable from that point on.
        return base
    except models.UnknownRunner as e:
        raise Stop(str(e))
    if name is None:
        raise Stop(f"no implementer runner configured for {repo!r} — repo_model "
                   f"has no enabled row for it or for 'default' (run "
                   f"`fleet-models set {repo} implementer <runner>`, or set the "
                   "'default' row)")
    base["EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER"] = name
    return base


def route_implementer(fm, cred, env=None):
    """Which runner carries this plan. Override first, zone rule LAST.

    The order is the guarantee. `EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER` is what makes a bakeoff arm
    a one-liner, and it is **subject to** the zone check rather than a way past
    it — so the check that can refuse comes last and a reader going top to bottom
    cannot get the ordering wrong.

    This function itself still falls back to `DEFAULT_RUNNER` when no override
    is present in `env` — but `main()` never lets it reach that path: it calls
    `resolved_implementer_env` first, which fills the override from
    `repo_model` or raises `Stop`. The fallback here exists for a caller
    (tests, a future direct invocation) that has no repo to resolve against.

    The zone rule is stated as an ALLOWLIST, per ADR-0006 §7: `zone: public`
    dispatches to a cloud backend; anything else — private, absent, malformed,
    mis-cased — resolves to a local runner or nothing. The opposite polarity
    (`if zone == "private": refuse`) sends a plan with no `zone:` line to a
    vendor, and `fleet-watch` forwards the field without validating it.

    The registry's `local` flag is the single local expression of that rule.
    Nothing here asks oracle: oracle is a completion gateway with one request and
    one reply and has no surface that names an agentic harness, so there is no
    oracle answer to honour about which runner to spawn (this supersedes plan
    0004 §3, and reverts the moment oracle gains a runner-aware route).

    `tier:` is deliberately not consulted. It is the PROVENANCE risk tier and
    drives review depth; oracle's identically-named cost tiers are a different
    axis, and conflating them would route a high-risk plan by its cost band."""
    env = os.environ if env is None else env
    name = (env.get("EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER") or "").strip() or DEFAULT_RUNNER
    runner = RUNNERS.get(name)
    if runner is None:
        raise Stop(f"EUNOMIA_IMPL_RUNNER={name!r} names no runner "
                   f"(known: {sorted(RUNNERS)})")
    if runner.needs and runner.needs != cred.kind:
        raise Stop(f"runner {name!r} needs a {runner.needs!r} credential and this "
                   f"run resolved {cred.kind!r} (initiator {cred.initiator!r}) — "
                   "refusing to serve it from the wrong credential")
    zone = (fm.get("zone") or env.get("EUNOMIA_PLAN_ZONE") or "").strip()
    if not runner.local and zone != "public":
        raise Stop(f"plan zone {zone!r} is not 'public' (ADR-0006 §7 is an "
                   f"allowlist), and runner {name!r} is not local — refusing to "
                   "send this plan's text to a cloud backend")
    return runner


# The child runs arbitrary shell, so this is an ALLOWLIST in the shape of
# `bin/fleet-candidate:_candidate_env` — not `fleet-watch`'s denylist. The
# reasoning is stated at bin/fleet-candidate:115 and applies here with the same
# force: enumerating what to withhold from a child that can run anything is the
# wrong way round, so anything not named below is simply absent.
#
# It matters most for FLEET_TOKEN_CMD. `fleet-watch:_child_env` pops it and its
# docstring calls the hazard "latent rather than live" because nothing reads it
# in a child yet — THIS PLAN IS THAT YET. On this fleet that variable names the
# admin-token helper, and an admin token can PATCH a branch-protection rule,
# push a `status: ready` plan and restore the rule: forging the ignition the
# whole system exists to make unforgeable.
#
# And that hazard is NOT closed, so it is not described as closed. The allowlist
# removes the VARIABLE, not the RECIPE: the admin helper is a fixed path taking
# no arguments, runnable by any process running as the operator with HOME — which
# this allowlist keeps — its name is written in docs/plan-dispatch.md inside the
# very worktree the session is told to read, and the session can run arbitrary
# commands. What would actually close it is the helper not being runnable on the
# implementer host, or gated on something the session does not have. That is the
# next plan's lane; it is named here so nobody inherits a control that reads as
# finished.
#
# EUNOMIA_SESSION is deliberately NOT here. It would be required the day
# `fleet-install-hooks` runs against the orchestrator's store — bin/fleet-prepush
# refuses a push whose branch lease is held by a different session, and without
# the variable the orchestrator's own lease would block the orchestrator's own
# push. Two things change together on that day, and the second is that
# FLEET_PUSH_OVERRIDE stays forbidden: it is the nearest fix a stuck session will
# find, and it disables row 6 entirely.
# SSH_AUTH_SOCK is here because WITHHOLDING IT WAS NOT A CONTROL, and its
# absence broke the one thing the implementer exists to do.
#
# Measured 2026-09-10. pr-proxy 0002-ci-logs ran for 700 seconds, closed both
# gaps in the plan, committed b4cfac4 — and could not push. Its own words:
#
#     ssh-add -l  ->  Could not open a connection to your authentication agent.
#     git push    ->  operator@forge.example: Permission denied (publickey).
#
# The commit sat in the shared store until a human rescued it. Every autonomous
# run would have ended the same way, so the fleet's output would have been
# orphaned commits rather than pull requests.
#
# And the variable's absence bought nothing. A child holding only this allowlist
# recovers the agent in one line, because the launchd socket directories are
# enumerable by the owner:
#
#     $ ls -1 /var/run/com.apple.launchd.*/Listeners | head -1
#     /var/run/com.apple.launchd.0r8RuJpHDF/Listeners
#     $ SSH_AUTH_SOCK=... ssh-add -l
#     256 SHA256:... opshost-automation-to-edgehost (ED25519)
#     $ SSH_AUTH_SOCK=... git ls-remote ssh://git@forge.example:2222/...
#     46cc74cf...  refs/heads/main
#
# That is the same argument the FLEET_TOKEN_CMD paragraph above makes and it
# lands the same way: the allowlist removes the VARIABLE, not the ACCESS. What
# it actually produced was an obstacle a determined child steps over and an
# honest one trips on — last night's session globbed /private/tmp instead of
# /var/run, guessed wrong, and stopped. Worst of both.
#
# SPEC principle 5 is what makes this the right call rather than a concession:
# identity and enforcement assume sessions run our own tooling honestly, and the
# ledger defends against races and forgetfulness rather than a malicious agent.
# A control that only stops the honest case is inverted.
#
# What WOULD narrow this is a push identity scoped to the one repo and the one
# branch — a per-dispatch deploy key, so the agent's other reach is not on the
# table at all. That is a real change, not a variable, and it is not this one.
_ENV_ALLOW = ("PATH", "HOME", "LANG", "LC_ALL", "LC_CTYPE", "TMPDIR", "TZ",
              "USER", "LOGNAME", "SHELL", "TERM", "SSH_AUTH_SOCK")


def implementer_env(runner, env=None):
    """The child's whole environment: the allowlist, plus what the runner needs."""
    src = os.environ if env is None else env
    out = {k: src[k] for k in _ENV_ALLOW if k in src}
    for k in runner.extra_env:
        if k in src:
            out[k] = src[k]
    return out


def harness_argv(runner):
    """The argv to spawn, or a hard stop that says which kind of absent it is.

    Two different faults, two different messages, neither a bare spawn OSError:
    a runner declared-but-unwired (`local-qwen`) reads nothing like a runner
    whose binary is missing from PATH, and an operator debugging at 3am needs to
    know which one they have."""
    if runner.unwired:
        raise Stop(f"runner {runner.name!r} is declared but not wired: {runner.unwired}")
    if not runner.argv:
        raise Stop(f"runner {runner.name!r} declares no harness command")
    exe = shutil.which(runner.argv[0], path=os.environ.get("PATH", os.defpath))
    if not exe:
        raise Stop(f"runner {runner.name!r} needs {runner.argv[0]!r}, which is not "
                   "on PATH on this host — refusing to spawn")
    return (exe,) + tuple(runner.argv[1:])


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the plan
_FM = re.compile(r"\A---\n(.*?)\n---\n", re.S)


def read_plan(worktree: Path, plan_path: str):
    """(front-matter dict, verbatim full text).

    The full text is returned UNPARSED and UNTOUCHED because it is copied into
    the implementer's prompt verbatim: plan 0004 makes that copy deterministic
    precisely so the Boundaries section — the plan's most load-bearing content —
    reaches the implementer without a model's summary in between."""
    p = (worktree / plan_path).resolve()
    # plan_path arrives from the watcher, which read it from the forge; resolve
    # and confine anyway. A path that escapes the worktree would read a file
    # from the operator's disk into a prompt.
    if not str(p).startswith(str(worktree.resolve()) + os.sep):
        raise ValueError(f"{plan_path!r} escapes the worktree")
    text = p.read_text()
    fm = {}
    m = _FM.match(text)
    if m:
        for line in m.group(1).splitlines():
            if ":" in line:
                k, v = line.split(":", 1)
                fm[k.strip()] = v.strip()
    return fm, text


PREAMBLE = BIN / "implementer-preamble.md"

IMPLEMENTER_TEMPLATE = """\
--- RUN FACTS ---
repository:  {repo}
worktree:    {worktree}
branch:      {branch}
plan file:   {plan_path}
PR marker:   Plan: {plan_id}
--- END RUN FACTS ---

You are implementing one feature plan in {repo}.

The plan below was merged to main by a human. That merge is the authorisation
for this work and is the only authorisation you have: implement what the plan
says, and nothing the plan does not say.

Read its Boundaries section as binding. Each boundary carries its reasoning
because the reasoning is what transfers to decisions the plan did not foresee.

Open your work as a pull request whose body carries the line `Plan: {plan_id}`,
and flip the plan file's `status:` to `done` in that same PR.

--- BEGIN PLAN ({plan_path}) ---
{plan_text}
--- END PLAN ---
"""


def read_preamble(path=None):
    """The environment briefing, read from a FILE and never built as a string.

    It is the operator's only window into what a headless session was told, and
    a file changes visibly in review where a formatted string does not — the
    same reasoning that will put the reviewer contract in `bin/reviewer-
    contract.md` when 0004's part 2b lands.

    A missing preamble is a hard stop, not an empty string. A run that quietly
    proceeded without the briefing would spawn a session that had been told
    nothing about its worktree, its token, or what it must not touch, and would
    look identical in every event and every log to a run that had."""
    path = Path(path) if path else PREAMBLE
    try:
        return path.read_text()
    except OSError as e:
        raise Stop(f"implementer preamble {path} unreadable "
                   f"({e.__class__.__name__}) — refusing to brief a session with "
                   "nothing")


def build_implementer_prompt(repo, plan_id, plan_path, plan_text, worktree,
                             branch, preamble_path=None):
    """Preamble verbatim, then fixed template, then the plan verbatim.

    Every byte of both files reaches the session unaltered: the plan is not
    summarised, trimmed, or template-substituted, because the Boundaries section
    arriving intact is this system's most load-bearing guarantee and a model
    asked to 'include the plan' is not one. Length is not a reason to change
    this. The per-run values live in the template's RUN FACTS block precisely so
    the preamble can stay byte-for-byte identical across runs and be diffed."""
    return read_preamble(preamble_path) + "\n" + IMPLEMENTER_TEMPLATE.format(
        repo=repo, plan_id=plan_id, plan_path=plan_path, plan_text=plan_text,
        worktree=worktree, branch=branch)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the branch
def lease_branch(lease_id):
    """The branch name, LEARNED from the lease record — never recomputed.

    `fleetlib.add_worktree` checks out `--detach` at origin/main, so the lease's
    branch does not exist in the tree yet and this wrapper creates it. It reads
    the name out of the lease rather than re-deriving `slug_of(plan_id)`: a
    second copy of the slug rule drifts, and the drift lands as a push the
    pre-push hook refuses (or, worse, does not) on a branch the lease does not
    cover."""
    rec = lib.read_lease(lease_id)
    if not rec:
        raise Stop(f"lease {lease_id} has no record — cannot learn the branch name")
    res = lib.resource_of(rec)
    if res.get("type") != "branch" or not res.get("branch"):
        raise Stop(f"lease {lease_id} is a {res.get('type')!r} lease, not a branch "
                   "lease — nothing to check out")
    return res["branch"]


def attach_branch(worktree, branch):
    """Put a RESUMED worktree on the existing branch, tracking origin.

    `add_worktree` attaches detached. A fix round has to commit and push to the
    branch the PR is open on, so the tree must be ON it — `-B` so a stale local
    ref from an earlier run is reset to origin rather than silently reused."""
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "checkout", "-B", branch, f"origin/{branch}"],
                       cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True,
                       timeout=120)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise Stop(f"could not attach to {branch}: {r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
    return branch


def create_branch(worktree, branch, log=None):
    """`git checkout -b` in the detached worktree. Never pushes.

    The push is the session's to make, with the implbot token it fetches
    itself; this wrapper holds no forge write path at all beyond reading
    (fleetlib makes the same guarantee for every workspace caller, ADR-0002).

    A SECOND RUN OF THE SAME PLAN could not create its branch (review 2369 M2).
    The ref lives in the shared store and survives `remove_worktree`, so a
    respawn got `fatal: a branch named 'feat/x' already exists`, exit 128, and
    every retry after it failed the same way — forever. Measured again
    2026-09-10: speakhush 0001 failed once on PATH, and the leftover ref then
    refused every subsequent dispatch.

    The fix is not `-B`. It is asking what an existing branch MEANS, because the
    three answers are genuinely different:

      on origin      REFUSE. Its work may be the marked PR that retires this
                     plan, and -B would reset over it.
      local only     Delete it with `-d` and carry on — but ONLY if git agrees
                     it is fully merged into HEAD (always origin/main here).
      undeletable    REFUSE. Either it carries unpushed commits or a worktree
                     holds it, and both are evidence.

    WHY `-d` AND NOT `-D` (review 2530 HIGH). The first cut used `-D` on the
    reasoning that a local-only branch is "an unreachable leftover; nothing
    points at it". That is false in the case this very file documents: on
    2026-09-10 pr-proxy 0002-ci-logs committed b4cfac4 and could not push, and
    the commit sat in the shared store reachable only through its branch. A run
    that commits and then dies on a controlled path — the 90-minute timeout, a
    push the pre-push hook rejects, the forge down at push time — leaves exactly
    that shape, and `main`'s `finally` removes the worktree while the ref
    survives. `-D` would delete the branch AND its reflog, leaving the commit
    reachable from nothing and findable only via `git fsck --lost-found` until
    gc. Silently: this function has no failure channel of its own.

    Measured on git 2.50.1 in a store built the way `add_worktree` builds one
    (bare origin, `clone --no-checkout`, `worktree add --detach origin/main`):

        branch with no commits     `-d` -> rc 0, "Deleted branch feat/empty"
        branch with one commit     `-d` -> rc 1, "not fully merged"
                                   `-D` -> rc 0, and the commit is then
                                           reachable from no ref at all

    So `-d` clears precisely the case the recovery exists for (speakhush 0001:
    branch created, session died on PATH, no commits) and refuses precisely the
    case that loses work."""
    # Asked as a ref question, not by matching git's stderr (review 2530 LOW).
    # `fatal: a branch named '%s' already exists` is localised, and the
    # orchestrator inherits LANG from the watcher — so on a non-English host the
    # recovery branch was never taken and the respawn regressed to the
    # fails-forever behaviour this function exists to fix.
    exists = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"refs/heads/{branch}"],
        cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    if exists.returncode != 0:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "checkout", "-b", branch], cwd=str(worktree),
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            raise Stop(f"could not create branch {branch}: "
                       f"{r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
        return branch

    on_origin = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"refs/remotes/origin/{branch}"],
        cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    if on_origin.returncode == 0:
        raise Stop(
            f"branch {branch} already exists ON ORIGIN — refusing to reset it. "
            "Its commits may be the marked pull request that retires this plan; "
            "resume the run instead of starting a second one over the top")

    gone = subprocess.run(["git", "branch", "-d", branch], cwd=str(worktree),
                          capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    if gone.returncode != 0:
        err = (gone.stderr or "").strip()
        # The two refusals are different problems with different remedies, and
        # the first cut named only the worktree one (review 2530 LOW) — so an
        # operator holding unpushed commits was told to go look at a tree that
        # may not exist.
        if "not fully merged" in err or "not fully merged" in (gone.stdout or ""):
            why = ("it carries commits that were never pushed — work from an "
                   "earlier run of this plan. `--resume` will NOT find them: it "
                   "checks out origin/<branch>, and they are not on origin. "
                   "Recover or discard them by hand before dispatching again")
        else:
            why = ("a worktree is holding it. If that tree is still on disk it "
                   "is the evidence of how the earlier run died; if its "
                   "directory is already gone, `git worktree prune` in the "
                   "store clears the claim")
        raise Stop(f"branch {branch} exists locally and would not delete "
                   f"({err[:120]}) — {why}. Refusing rather than destroying it")

    # Noted, because a ref disappearing with no record is how a later reader
    # loses the thread. `-d` succeeding means git agreed it held nothing.
    if log is not None:
        log.note("branch-cleared", branch=branch,
                 detail=(gone.stdout or "").strip()[:80] or "-")

    again = subprocess.run(["git", "checkout", "-b", branch], cwd=str(worktree),
                           capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
    if again.returncode != 0:
        raise Stop(f"could not create branch {branch} after clearing a dead "
                   f"local ref: {again.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
    return branch


# --------------------------------------------------------------- the session
Session = collections.namedtuple(
    "Session", "exit_code timed_out transcript shapes seconds reads backend_error",
    defaults=(None,))

BACKEND_MESSAGE_CHARS = 120


def read_backend_error(text):
    """`{status, message}` from the LAST `result` record in a stream-json
    transcript that ended on a model-backend error, else None (plan 0079 D1).

    Read from the in-memory `text` before it is written, never by reopening the
    transcript: mopsus classifies `failure_reason` only, so the evidence has to
    ride in the reason, and only in a redacted, bounded form. The message is
    redacted BEFORE it is cut, so a token cannot be split by the cut into a
    shape the patterns no longer match. A stream with no such record (another
    harness, a plain-text one) yields None and the caller's behaviour is
    unchanged."""
    last = None
    for line in (text or "").splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line.startswith("{"):
            continue
        try:
            rec = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("type") == "result":
            last = rec
    if not last or not last.get("is_error") or last.get("api_error_status") is None:
        return None
    msg = last.get("result")
    msg = redact_transcript(msg if isinstance(msg, str) else "")[0]
    msg = " ".join(msg.split())[:BACKEND_MESSAGE_CHARS]
    return {"status": last["api_error_status"], "message": msg}


def work_began(repo, branch, worktree):
    """Whether the lane's branch has any commit beyond the base it was cut from,
    locally or on the forge. False ONLY when both were checked and both are
    empty; a check that fails is "could not tell", and that counts as work
    (plan 0079 D2) — requeuing a session that pushed is the one thing
    ADR-0012 §1 forbids."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "rev-list", "--count", f"origin/main..{branch}"],
            cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        if r.returncode != 0 or not r.stdout.strip().isdigit():
            return True
        if int(r.stdout.strip()) > 0:
            return True
        tip = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{branch}^{{commit}}"],
                             cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True,
                             timeout=60)
        base = tip.stdout.strip()
        if tip.returncode != 0 or not base:
            return True
        _mod, forge = _forge()
        st, body = forge.get_branch(repo, branch)
        if st == 404:
            return False
        commit = (body or {}).get("commit") if isinstance(body, dict) else None
        head = commit.get("id") if isinstance(commit, dict) else None
        if st != 200 or not head:
            return True
        return head != base
    except Exception:
        return True


_RESOLVED_IMPL_TIMEOUT = None   # (minutes, source), resolved ONCE per run


def implementer_timeout_minutes(repo=None):
    """This run's wall-clock minutes, resolved ONCE and cached module-wide.

    The first call wins. In production that is main()'s, with the real repo,
    made before the first session is spawned — every later call, including the
    three that quote it in a comment, returns that SAME value even if
    config/impl-bounds.conf is edited mid-run (plan 0042 §2/§4). Malformed or
    missing is a Stop, never a quiet 90 — see impl_bound()."""
    global _RESOLVED_IMPL_TIMEOUT
    if _RESOLVED_IMPL_TIMEOUT is None:
        _RESOLVED_IMPL_TIMEOUT = impl_bound(repo)
    return _RESOLVED_IMPL_TIMEOUT[0]


def implementer_timeout_source():
    """'repo-entry' / 'conf-default', for the run that resolved a bound."""
    return _RESOLVED_IMPL_TIMEOUT[1] if _RESOLVED_IMPL_TIMEOUT else None


def _self_peak_kb():
    """This process's own peak RSS in KILOBYTES, platform normalised.

    `ru_maxrss` is BYTES on Darwin and KILOBYTES on Linux. Getting that wrong is
    a silent 1024x error in whichever direction, and this fleet runs both — the
    orchestrator on opshost and the suite on the cihost-linux CI label."""
    kb = resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss
    return kb // 1024 if sys.platform == "darwin" else kb


class _PeakRSS(threading.Thread):
    """Peak resident memory of the implementer's whole process GROUP.

    WHY THE GROUP, not the child. `claude -p` has grandchildren — that is not a
    guess, it is why `_kill_group` exists below, after one of them opened a
    marked PR seconds behind a wrapper that had already declared failure. The
    memory question is about that whole tree, and the process that happens to
    lead it is not the only thing resident.

    `start_new_session=True` on the Popen makes the child a process-group
    LEADER, so its pid IS the pgid. No `os.getpgid` call, and no window where
    the group does not exist yet.

    WHY SAMPLED, and what that costs. `os.wait4`'s `ru_maxrss` would be exact,
    but reading it means replacing `proc.wait()` in the timeout/kill-group path
    — code carrying scar tissue from precisely the grandchild case above. A
    measurement is not worth destabilising that. So this samples, and the number
    is therefore a FLOOR on the true peak: a spike falling entirely between two
    samples is invisible. It is reported as `peak_rss_mb`, never `max_rss_mb`,
    for that reason. Do not quote it as an exact maximum.

    Never raises into the caller. `ps` failing, the group exiting mid-read, a
    row that is not an integer — all of it means "no sample", because a resource
    number must not be able to fail a plan that otherwise worked.
    """

    def __init__(self, pgid, interval=2.0):
        super().__init__(daemon=True)
        self.pgid = pgid
        self.interval = interval
        self.peak_kb = 0
        self.samples = 0
        self._stop = threading.Event()

    def _sample(self):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "rss=", "-g", str(self.pgid)],
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=5)
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            return
        total = 0
        for field in (r.stdout or "").split():
            try:
                total += int(field)
            except ValueError:
                continue           # a header or a torn read, not a failure
        if total:
            self.samples += 1
            self.peak_kb = max(self.peak_kb, total)

    def run(self):
        # Sample once immediately: a session that dies in under `interval`
        # seconds should still produce a number rather than a silent zero.
        self._sample()
        while not self._stop.wait(self.interval):
            self._sample()

    def stop(self):
        self._stop.set()
        # One last look before the group is reaped. On a fast exit this is
        # often the only sample that saw anything at all.
        self._sample()

    @property
    def peak_mb(self):
        """MB, or None when nothing was sampled — which is NOT zero.

        Zero reads as "it used no memory"; None reads as "not measured". The two
        have to stay distinguishable, or the first person to chart this charts a
        floor of zeros as real data."""
        return round(self.peak_kb / 1024) if self.samples else None


def _kill_group(proc):
    """SIGTERM then SIGKILL to the process GROUP, not the child.

    `subprocess.run(timeout=)` kills the direct child, and `claude -p` has
    grandchildren. A grandchild surviving the timeout can open the marked PR
    AFTER the wrapper has declared failure — and the plan then dedupes as done
    on a PR nothing verified, forever, with an orphaned lease. That is why the
    session is spawned with `start_new_session=True` and why this signals the
    group id."""
    try:
        pgid = os.getpgid(proc.pid)
    except OSError:
        return
    # REFUSE to signal our own group (2369 L1). The child is spawned with
    # start_new_session=True so its pgid is its own pid — but if that had not
    # taken effect, its group would be OURS, and the SIGKILL below would take
    # down the orchestrator mid-run. That was survivable while this ran only on
    # timeout; it runs on every exit now, so it would be every run.
    if pgid == os.getpgrp():
        warn(f"refusing to signal process group {pgid}: it is this "
             "orchestrator's own — start_new_session did not take effect")
        return
    try:
        os.killpg(pgid, signal.SIGTERM)
    except OSError:
        return
    try:
        proc.wait(timeout=10)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        pass
    # SIGKILL the GROUP unconditionally, even when the direct child has already
    # exited. The child exiting says nothing about a grandchild that trapped
    # SIGTERM and kept running, and the grandchild is the process that can still
    # open the marked PR after this wrapper has declared failure. An earlier
    # version returned as soon as the child was reaped and left exactly that
    # process alive; ProcessLookupError here simply means the group is gone.
    try:
        os.killpg(pgid, signal.SIGKILL)
    except OSError:
        pass
    try:
        proc.wait(timeout=10)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        pass


def run_implementer(worktree, prompt, cred, route, lease_id, log=None, tag=None):
    """Spawn the routed session on the built prompt, and always write a
    transcript.

    Shape, and why each part of it is load-bearing:

    * the prompt goes in on **stdin**, verbatim, never in argv — argv is
      world-readable in `ps`, and this prompt contains the whole plan;
    * the child gets `implementer_env(route)` and nothing else;
    * `start_new_session=True` puts it in its own process group, so a timeout
      can kill the grandchildren `claude -p` spawns;
    * output is read by a thread into a list of chunks and **joined before
      redaction** — see `redact_transcript`;
    * the transcript is written whatever happened, including on timeout, because
      the run that went wrong is the one worth reading.

    Returns a Session. It deliberately does NOT decide success: a zero exit
    proves nothing here, and `verify_marked_pr` is the guarantee."""
    argv = harness_argv(route)
    env = implementer_env(route)
    mins = implementer_timeout_minutes()
    if log:
        log.note("implementer-start", runner=route.name, credential=cred.kind,
                 timeout_min=mins, timeout_source=implementer_timeout_source(),
                 cwd=worktree)
    started = time.time()
    proc = subprocess.Popen(argv, cwd=str(worktree), env=env,
                            stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                            stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, start_new_session=True)
    # start_new_session=True above makes proc.pid the group leader's pid, hence
    # the pgid. See _PeakRSS on why the group rather than the child.
    peak = _PeakRSS(proc.pid)
    peak.start()
    chunks = []

    def _drain():
        # read1, not read: `read(n)` on a BufferedReader blocks until it has n
        # bytes or EOF, which would hide the very case redact_transcript exists
        # for. read1 hands back whatever the pipe has, so a credential really can
        # arrive split across two reads — and the suite pins that it does
        # (Session.reads), because a control tested only against input it never
        # sees is not tested.
        try:
            for chunk in iter(lambda: proc.stdout.read1(65536), b""):
                chunks.append(chunk)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass

    reader = threading.Thread(target=_drain, daemon=True)
    reader.start()
    try:
        proc.stdin.write(prompt.encode("utf-8"))
        proc.stdin.close()
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        # a harness that died before reading its prompt: the wait below reports
        # the exit code, and the transcript carries whatever it managed to say
        pass
    timed_out = False
    try:
        rc = proc.wait(timeout=mins * 60)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        timed_out = True
        _kill_group(proc)
        rc = proc.returncode if proc.returncode is not None else -signal.SIGKILL
    else:
        # Reap the group on a NORMAL exit too (2369 L1). A session that detaches
        # a child and exits 0 leaves that child able to push AFTER the verdict is
        # read — the same race _kill_group's own docstring describes for the
        # timeout path, arriving through the door nobody was watching. The
        # timeout branch above already ran it, hence `else`.
        _kill_group(proc)
    peak.stop()
    reader.join(timeout=30)
    text = b"".join(chunks).decode("utf-8", "replace")
    path, shapes = write_transcript(lease_id, text, tag)
    seconds = int(time.time() - started)
    # RUSAGE_SELF is the wrapper's OWN high-water mark, and it IS exact — unlike
    # the sampled group figure. Reported separately rather than summed: adding an
    # exact number to a floor yields a number that is neither.
    self_mb = round(_self_peak_kb() / 1024)
    if log:
        log.note("implementer-exit", rc=rc, timed_out=timed_out, seconds=seconds,
                 transcript=path, shapes=",".join(shapes) or "-",
                 peak_rss_mb=("-" if peak.peak_mb is None else peak.peak_mb),
                 rss_samples=peak.samples, orch_rss_mb=self_mb)
    if shapes:
        warn(f"transcript {path} carried credential shapes {sorted(shapes)} — "
             "redacted on write")
    return Session(rc, timed_out, path, shapes, seconds, len(chunks),
                   read_backend_error(text))


# ---------------------------------------------------------- the verification
def _forgejo_base_url():
    return os.environ.get("FLEET_FORGEJO_URL", "http://forge.example:3000").rstrip("/")


class TokenError(Exception):
    """A resolver miss, named by role and configuration key.

    Never built from the helper's stdout or stderr (plan 0055 boundary): that
    text can quote the token itself — `fleetforge.Forge._request` discards the
    text of every exception it catches for the same reason, since the standard
    library's HTTP client raises with the token embedded in the header it
    failed to send."""


# fleet-watch/fleet-orphans/fleet-candidate resolve their one credential from
# FLEET_TOKEN_CMD, a read-only position: whatever that command mints is free
# to be reconfigured per deployment because nothing downstream of it can act
# as an account, only read as one. "authoring" is a different position — this
# wrapper posts comments and edits pull request bodies with what this
# resolves, and is graded on the review that identity receives — so it gets
# its own configuration key rather than sharing the reading one. Configuring
# only the reading key must never silently supply this one, or a deployment
# that only meant to change what fleet-watch reads with would change who the
# orchestrator writes as.
_TOKEN_ROLES = {
    "authoring": ("FLEET_AUTHOR_TOKEN_CMD", "~/bin/fetch-forgejo-token.sh"),
}


def token_for(role):
    """The Forgejo token for `role`, via a command configurable per role.

    Both the env var and the default live in `_TOKEN_ROLES`, not inlined at a
    `subprocess.run` call site — that inlining was plan 0055's finding: this
    file and fleet-reviews were the two places on the fleet still hardcoding
    the helper's path, so a deployment whose secrets come from somewhere else
    had to edit code rather than configuration."""
    try:
        env_key, default = _TOKEN_ROLES[role]
    except KeyError:
        raise TokenError(f"token_for(role={role!r}): no such role")
    cmd = os.environ.get(env_key, "").strip() or os.path.expanduser(default)
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["sh", "-c", cmd], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        raise TokenError(f"token_for(role={role!r}) via {env_key}: helper "
                         f"did not run ({e.__class__.__name__})")
    lines = [l.strip() for l in r.stdout.splitlines() if l.strip()]
    if r.returncode != 0 or not lines:
        raise TokenError(f"token_for(role={role!r}) via {env_key}: helper "
                         "produced no token")
    return lines[-1]


def _implbot_token():
    """The identity this wrapper authors as — every forge call in this file,
    read or write, goes through it. Kept as its own name, wrapping
    token_for("authoring"), so a caller never has to spell the role out."""
    try:
        return token_for("authoring")
    except TokenError as e:
        raise Stop(f"{e} — the forge action cannot proceed")


def forge_timeout(env=None):
    """Seconds to allow one forge request. MEASURED, not guessed.

    `fleetforge.Forge` defaults to 20s, and that is below what this fleet's own
    listing costs. On 2026-09-09, `pulls?state=all&limit=50` against
    operator/eunomia took **22.6s** for a single page — Forgejo does roughly
    0.45s of work per pull request on that endpoint and the repo has 247 of
    them. Measured side by side, curl and urllib agreed: 23.7s and 22.7s.

    So the read did not blip. It failed EVERY time, deterministically, by
    sitting a couple of seconds over the client's patience — which is why the
    retry added in #246 could not rescue it: all five attempts hit the same
    wall. Two complete dispatches were discarded this way (eunomia #245,
    speakhush #161), and both were misdiagnosed as transient.

    60s is ~3x the measured cost. It is not a fix for the cost itself: a full
    scan is still ~22s per page, so verification can take minutes on a large
    repo. Making the lookup cheaper — the PR being sought is the newest one, so
    a sorted first page would usually answer without paging at all — is a
    separate change and is noted in docs/forge.md."""
    env = os.environ if env is None else env
    raw = (env.get("FLEET_FORGE_TIMEOUT") or "").strip()
    if not raw:
        return 60
    try:
        n = int(raw)
    except ValueError:
        raise Stop(f"FLEET_FORGE_TIMEOUT={raw!r} is not an integer")
    if n < 1:
        raise Stop(f"FLEET_FORGE_TIMEOUT={n} — a forge read needs some patience")
    return n


def _forge():
    mod = _load_sibling("fleetforge.py", "_fleetforge")
    return mod, mod.Forge(_forgejo_base_url(), _implbot_token(),
                          timeout=forge_timeout())


def implementer_accounts():
    """uids UNION logins, with the author role's own account always in the
    login set.

    The union is `fleet-watch`'s rule and is repeated for its reason, not its
    shape: giving uids precedence meant an operator adding a second
    implementer's uid — and forgetting the author account's own login — made
    every login-authored marked PR invisible. `lib.agent_roles()["author"]`
    guarantees that membership at construction (plan 0056's `lib._role`), not
    by this caller remembering to union it in."""
    uids = {x.strip() for x in os.environ.get("FLEET_IMPLEMENTER_UIDS", "").split(",")
            if x.strip()}
    return uids, lib.agent_roles()["author"].logins


def _plan_status_at(mod, forge, repo, plan_path, ref):
    """The plan file's `status:` on a ref, or None when it cannot be read.

    Read through the contents API rather than diffed: `status: done` at the PR's
    head IS the flip, because the watcher only ever dispatches a plan that was
    `ready` on main. None means unreadable, which the caller must not treat as
    'not done'."""
    st, body = forge.get_contents(repo, plan_path, ref=ref)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict):
        return None
    raw = body.get("content") or ""
    try:
        text = base64.b64decode(raw).decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return None
    m = _FM.match(text)
    if not m:
        return None
    for line in m.group(1).splitlines():
        if line.split(":", 1)[0].strip() == "status" and ":" in line:
            return line.split(":", 1)[1].strip()
    return None


# Five attempts across ten minutes, doubling. The sleeps sum to exactly 600s,
# so the last read happens ten minutes after the first — long enough to outlive
# a forge restart, a network blip, or a reboot of the box serving it.
READ_ATTEMPTS = 5
READ_BACKOFF = (40, 80, 160, 320)


# Patched by the test wiring so the suite exercises the real retry policy
# without spending ten minutes per failing-read case. Production never rebinds
# it; a test that forgets to is a test that hangs, which is loud enough.
_sleep = time.sleep


def read_pulls(mod, forge, repo, log=None, sleep=None):
    """Every pull request on `repo`, retried. Returns (pulls, None) or
    (None, outcome).

    A transient read must not discard a run. On 2026-09-09 the first real
    dispatch produced a complete, correct PR — 654 lines of implementation, 32
    tests, the marker present and the plan flipped — and this listing failed
    ONCE, 39 seconds later. The wrapper reported "could not read pull requests"
    and stopped, throwing away 34 minutes of model time over one HTTP blip. The
    same read succeeded on every attempt minutes afterwards.

    The rule that unreadable is never 'no' was right and is unchanged. What was
    wrong was treating unreadable as terminal on the first try, at the exact
    moment the run had the most to lose.

    `sleep` is injectable so the tests exercise the real backoff without
    spending ten minutes doing it."""
    sleep = sleep or _sleep
    outcome = None
    for attempt in range(READ_ATTEMPTS):
        pulls, pages = [], mod.paged(forge.list_pulls, repo, state="all", cap=20)
        for page in pages:
            pulls.extend(page)
        if pages.outcome == mod.EXHAUSTED:
            if attempt and log is not None:
                log.note("pulls-read-recovered", attempt=attempt + 1)
            return pulls, None
        outcome = pages.outcome
        if attempt < len(READ_BACKOFF):
            if log is not None:
                log.note("pulls-read-retry", attempt=attempt + 1,
                         outcome=outcome, sleep=READ_BACKOFF[attempt])
            warn(f"could not read {repo} pull requests ({outcome}) — "
                 f"attempt {attempt + 1}/{READ_ATTEMPTS}, retrying in "
                 f"{READ_BACKOFF[attempt]}s")
            sleep(READ_BACKOFF[attempt])
    return None, outcome


def stamp_marker(mod, forge, repo, num, body, marker, log=None):
    """Append `marker` to PR `num`'s body. (ok, detail).

    ONLY called once the PR has been proven to be this run's own — implementer
    author, head is this lease's branch, base is main. Those three checks run
    BEFORE this in verify_marked_pr and are the licence for writing to it; a
    stamp that ran earlier would be editing a pull request on the strength of a
    branch name.

    WHY THE ORCHESTRATOR AND NOT THE PROMPT. The preamble asks for the marker
    twice, and models produce it wrong. Measured: pr-proxy #42 and #79 carry the
    plan as a PATH (`Plan: plans/0001-merge-queue.md`) so the line-anchored
    matcher never saw it and merged work stayed re-dispatchable; speakhush #162,
    the fleet's first fully autonomous PR, carries no marker at all and its 301
    lines of finished work were invisible to dedupe. Two failures, two different
    shapes, from the same hope. The orchestrator knows the id and can write it.

    This is the same move commit-identity already made and for the same stated
    reason: a guarantee written as prompt text is a hope.

    APPENDS. The body is the implementer's report and a reader's first account of
    the change; replacing it to satisfy a matcher would destroy the more valuable
    thing to fix the cheaper one.

    Verifies by READING BACK. A 200 on the PATCH is not evidence the body
    changed — the same reason forgejo-create-repo re-reads its protection rule."""
    new = (body.rstrip() + "\n\n" + marker + "\n") if body.strip() else marker + "\n"
    st, _ = forge.edit_pull_body(repo, num, new)
    if st is None or not (200 <= int(st) < 300):
        return False, f"edit returned {st}"
    st2, fresh = forge.get_pull(repo, num)
    if st2 is None or not isinstance(fresh, dict):
        return False, f"stamped, but the read-back returned {st2}"
    if marker not in (fresh.get("body") or ""):
        return False, "the edit reported success and the body does not carry it"
    if log is not None:
        log.note("marker-stamped", pr=num, marker=marker)
    return True, "stamped"


def verify_marked_pr(repo, branch, plan_id, plan_path, forge=None, mod=None,
                     log=None):
    """Five checks, from the forge, run whatever the session reported.

    All five or the run stops: a PR exists, authored by an implementer account,
    head is this lease's branch, base is `main`, its body carries
    `Plan: <plan_id>`, and the plan file at its head says `status: done`.

    Unreadable is never 'no'. A PR listing that fails or hits its page cap fails
    the verification with that as the reason, rather than reporting 'no PR' — a
    truncated read that reads as absence would let a run fail while its work
    exists, and (once 0011 lands) comment on it as if nothing had happened.

    Returns (ok, reason, pr_number)."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    marker = f"Plan: {plan_id}"
    uids, logins = implementer_accounts()
    pulls, outcome = read_pulls(mod, forge, repo)
    if pulls is None:
        return False, (f"could not read {repo} pull requests ({outcome}) after "
                       f"{READ_ATTEMPTS} attempts over "
                       f"{sum(READ_BACKOFF) // 60} minutes"), None
    ours = [pr for pr in pulls
            if ((pr.get("head") or {}).get("ref") or "") == branch]
    if not ours:
        return False, f"no pull request with head {branch}", None
    marked = [pr for pr in ours if marker in (pr.get("body") or "")]
    pr = (marked or ours)[0]
    num = pr.get("number")
    user = pr.get("user") or {}
    if not (str(user.get("id")) in uids or (user.get("login") or "").lower() in logins):
        return False, (f"#{num} on {branch} was opened by "
                       f"{user.get('login') or '?'}, not an implementer account"), num
    base = (pr.get("base") or {}).get("ref") or ""
    if base != "main":
        return False, f"#{num} targets {base!r}, not main", num
    if not marked:
        # Everything above has proven this PR is ours: implementer author, head
        # is this lease's branch, base is main. So write the marker rather than
        # abandoning finished work for the want of one line.
        ok, why = stamp_marker(mod, forge, repo, num, pr.get("body") or "",
                               marker, log)
        if not ok:
            return False, (f"#{num} carries no {marker!r} line and it could not "
                           f"be added ({why}) — an unmarked PR leaves the plan "
                           "re-dispatchable with its work in a branch nobody "
                           "will look at"), num
    head_sha = (pr.get("head") or {}).get("sha") or branch
    status = _plan_status_at(mod, forge, repo, plan_path, head_sha)
    if status is None:
        return False, f"#{num}: {plan_path} unreadable at {str(head_sha)[:7]}", num
    if status != "done":
        return False, f"#{num}: {plan_path} still says status: {status}", num
    return True, "", num


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the review loop
# Bounds live in a conf file, not here, because a tier-3 repo deserves more
# rounds than a docs repo. The CEILING lives here because a config integer must
# never be able to mean "spend until someone notices".
REVIEW_BOUND_CEILING = 10
DEFAULT_REVIEW_BOUND = 5
REVIEW_BOUNDS = BIN.parent / "config" / "review-bounds.conf"
REVIEWER_CONTRACT = BIN / "reviewer-contract.md"

# Machine-readable anchors so a successor process can find its own prior rounds
# in the thread. The ledger is posted to the PR rather than kept in a local file
# for exactly that reason (plan 0011 §2).
LEDGER_MARKER = "<!-- eunomia:review-ledger -->"
DEFERRED_MARKER = "<!-- eunomia:deferred-findings -->"


def review_bound(repo, path=None):
    """Rounds allowed for this repo. Per-repo, clamped, never unlimited.

    An unparseable line is a hard stop in the shape config/secret-patterns.conf
    established: a bound that silently reverts to the default is an unattended
    process spending more than the operator set. 0 is refused rather than read
    as infinity — that conventional misreading is the one that costs money."""
    path = Path(path) if path else REVIEW_BOUNDS
    values = {}
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError as e:
        raise Stop(f"review bounds {path} unreadable ({e.__class__.__name__}) — "
                   "refusing to run an unbounded fix loop")
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        if "=" not in line:
            raise Stop(f"unparseable line in {path}: {line!r} — expected "
                       "'<owner/repo|default> = <int>'")
        key, _, raw = line.partition("=")
        try:
            n = int(raw.strip())
        except ValueError:
            raise Stop(f"non-integer bound in {path} for {key.strip()!r}: "
                       f"{raw.strip()!r}")
        if n < 1:
            raise Stop(f"bound for {key.strip()!r} in {path} is {n}; there is no "
                       "value meaning unlimited and 0 is not one")
        values[key.strip()] = n
    n = values.get(repo, values.get("default", DEFAULT_REVIEW_BOUND))
    if n > REVIEW_BOUND_CEILING:
        warn(f"review bound {n} for {repo} exceeds ceiling "
             f"{REVIEW_BOUND_CEILING} — clamped")
        return REVIEW_BOUND_CEILING
    return n


# ------------------------------------------------------ the implementer bound
# Per repo, in the same shape as REVIEW_BOUNDS above, and committed like it. The
# CEILING lives here for the same reason: a config integer must never be able
# to mean "spend until someone notices" (plan 0042 §3).
IMPL_BOUND_CEILING = 240
IMPL_BOUNDS = BIN.parent / "config" / "impl-bounds.conf"


def impl_bound(repo, path=None):
    """(minutes, source) for this repo's implementer wall-clock bound.

    Deliberately does NOT mirror review_bound all the way: there is no
    DEFAULT_IMPL_BOUND constant to fall back on. A conf with no entry for
    `repo` AND no `default` line is a Stop, not the built-in 90 — the default
    has exactly one home, and it is the conf's `default` line (plan 0042 §3).
    A conf whose `default` line was lost to a bad edit is the same
    broken-checkout evidence a missing file is."""
    path = Path(path) if path else IMPL_BOUNDS
    values = {}
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError as e:
        raise Stop(f"implementer bounds {path} unreadable ({e.__class__.__name__}) "
                   "— refusing to run an unbounded implementer session")
    for line in text.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line or line.startswith("#"):
            continue
        if "=" not in line:
            raise Stop(f"unparseable line in {path}: {line!r} — expected "
                       "'<owner/repo|default> = <int>'")
        key, _, raw = line.partition("=")
        try:
            n = int(raw.strip())
        except ValueError:
            raise Stop(f"non-integer bound in {path} for {key.strip()!r}: "
                       f"{raw.strip()!r}")
        if n < 1:
            raise Stop(f"bound for {key.strip()!r} in {path} is {n}; there is no "
                       "value meaning unlimited and 0 is not one")
        values[key.strip()] = n
    if repo in values:
        n, source = values[repo], "repo-entry"
    elif "default" in values:
        n, source = values["default"], "conf-default"
    else:
        raise Stop(f"{path} has no entry for {repo!r} and no 'default' line — "
                   "refusing to guess an implementer bound")
    if n > IMPL_BOUND_CEILING:
        warn(f"implementer bound {n} for {repo} exceeds ceiling "
             f"{IMPL_BOUND_CEILING} — clamped")
        return IMPL_BOUND_CEILING, source
    return n, source


def read_reviewer_contract(repo, pr, sha, plan_path, path=None):
    """The mandate, from a FILE, with exactly four substitutions.

    Nothing this wrapper knows about the run may be added. The wrapper is graded
    on approved, so a mandate it authors lets it steer the reviewer away from its
    own weak spots without ever touching the reviewer's credentials."""
    path = Path(path) if path else REVIEWER_CONTRACT
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError as e:
        raise Stop(f"reviewer contract {path} unreadable "
                   f"({e.__class__.__name__}) — refusing to dispatch a review "
                   "with no mandate")
    return (text.replace("{repo}", str(repo)).replace("{pr}", str(pr))
                .replace("{sha}", str(sha)).replace("{plan_path}", str(plan_path)))


def review_command():
    """The review dispatcher, as a COMMAND seam.

    The orchestrator never holds the reviewer's credential — it invokes a command
    that posts as revbot and holds that token itself. That is coder ≠
    reviewer at the credential level, and it is also why replacing the reviewer
    is configuration rather than a rewrite."""
    raw = os.environ.get("FLEET_REVIEW_CMD", "").strip()
    if raw:
        return shlex.split(raw)
    return [sys.executable, os.path.expanduser("~/agent/dispatch-review.py")]


# CI is a WAIT, not a failure. `dispatch-review` refuses a head whose checks are
# still running — correctly, since reviewing a commit that may not compile wastes
# a review. The loop treated that refusal as terminal, so on 2026-09-09 it pushed
# a fix and asked for a review three seconds later, was refused, and gave up with
# the work sitting unreviewed on #245.
CI_POLL_SECS = 30
CI_WAIT_SECS = 1800
# `warning` is a RESOLVED combined state in Forgejo, not a pending one. Left
# out, it was waited out for the full bound and then reported as unresolved.
CI_RESOLVED = ("success", "failure", "error", "warning")
# How many consecutive unreadable status reads to tolerate before giving up.
CI_READ_ATTEMPTS = 5

# The CI-fix briefing's own wait for a stored log (plan 0069 §3): one
# fleet-collect sweep interval (120 s, launchd/org.eunomia.fleet-collect.plist)
# plus its own 90 s budget (bin/fleet-ci's BUDGET_SECONDS) — a failure resolved
# a moment ago may have no ci_task row at all until the next sweep. Polled, not
# fetched: the orchestrator never dials Forgejo or a runner for this, and never
# calls fleet-ci's sync() — it waits for the collector fleet-collect already
# runs on its own cadence.
CI_LOG_POLL_SECS = 15
CI_LOG_POLL_BOUND = 210


def ci_status(repo, sha, forge=None, mod=None):
    """(state, failed_contexts, n_statuses). n_statuses is -1 when UNREADABLE.

    The count carries readability, not the state string, because Forgejo
    returns `state: ""` for a commit with no statuses — MEASURED on
    operator/ladon, 2026-09-09. An earlier version collapsed that empty string to
    None and read it as unreadable, so `review_loop` would have failed at round
    one on every repo without CI. Its test missed it by serving `pending` with
    an empty list, a shape the forge never produces (review 2441 HIGH).

    None as the state now means only "readable, and Forgejo gave no verdict",
    which -1 vs 0 tells apart."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    st, body = forge.get_commit_status(repo, sha)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict):
        return None, [], -1
    statuses = body.get("statuses") or []
    state = (body.get("state") or "").lower() or None
    failed = sorted({(s.get("context") or "?") for s in statuses
                     if (s.get("status") or s.get("state") or "").lower()
                     in ("failure", "error")})
    return state, failed, len(statuses)


def await_ci(repo, sha, forge=None, mod=None, log=None, sleep=None,
             bound=None, poll=None):
    """Block until CI on `sha` resolves. Returns (state, failed_contexts).

    Returns the resolved state — `failure` included, which is a real answer and
    the caller's problem. `None` means unreadable or never resolved.
    """
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    sleep = sleep or _sleep
    bound = CI_WAIT_SECS if bound is None else bound
    poll = CI_POLL_SECS if poll is None else poll
    waited, unreadable, empty_seen = 0, 0, 0
    settled_n, settled_seen = None, 0
    while True:
        state, failed, n = ci_status(repo, sha, forge, mod)

        if n < 0:
            # Unreadable is never green — but it is also not instantly fatal.
            # read_pulls in this same file retries a flaky forge read; ending
            # the whole loop on one bad response while its sibling retries was
            # an inconsistency, not a policy (review 2441).
            unreadable += 1
            if unreadable > CI_READ_ATTEMPTS or waited >= bound:
                if log is not None:
                    log.note("ci-unreadable", sha=str(sha)[:8],
                             attempts=unreadable)
                return None, []
            sleep(poll)
            waited += poll
            continue
        unreadable = 0

        if n == 0:
            # Nothing configured to run — green by absence. But NOT on sight:
            # a commit pushed a second ago has no statuses yet either, and
            # Forgejo creates the pending row shortly after. Confirming the
            # emptiness one poll apart tells "no CI here" from "CI has not
            # started", which the fix round then dispatches into (review 2441).
            empty_seen += 1
            if empty_seen >= 2 or waited >= bound:
                if log is not None:
                    log.note("ci-none", sha=str(sha)[:8], polls=empty_seen)
                return "success", []
            sleep(poll)
            waited += poll
            continue
        empty_seen = 0

        if state in CI_RESOLVED:
            # RESOLVED is not SETTLED, and the difference costs a review round.
            #
            # Forgejo fires workflows off PATH filters, so the set of statuses a
            # commit will eventually carry is not knowable in advance and does
            # not arrive at once. `Plans / lint` runs only when the push diff
            # touches plans/**, and on 2026-09-10 it reported FAILURE on
            # eunomia#252 seconds after the four `CI /` contexts had gone green.
            # A review was dispatched against that momentary green, and
            # revbot APPROVED a pull request whose CI was red.
            #
            # This is the same distinction the empty case above already draws —
            # "nothing configured" versus "nothing started yet" — applied to the
            # case where something DID report. Requiring the status COUNT to
            # hold still across two polls turns "nothing is red right now" into
            # "nothing is red and nothing new is still arriving". A late red is
            # then seen here, where it costs one poll, instead of by a reviewer,
            # where it costs a round and a false approval.
            #
            # Deliberately the count and not the contexts: a workflow that
            # re-reports the same context is not new information, and comparing
            # sets would make a rerun look like an arrival forever.
            if n == settled_n:
                settled_seen += 1
            else:
                settled_n, settled_seen = n, 1
            if settled_seen >= 2 or waited >= bound:
                if waited and log is not None:
                    log.note("ci-resolved", sha=str(sha)[:8], state=state,
                             waited=waited, checks=n)
                return state, failed
            sleep(poll)
            waited += poll
            continue
        if waited >= bound:
            if log is not None:
                log.note("ci-timeout", sha=str(sha)[:8], waited=waited,
                         state=state)
            return None, []
        if log is not None and waited == 0:
            log.note("ci-waiting", sha=str(sha)[:8], state=state)
        sleep(poll)
        waited += poll


def dispatch_review(repo, pr, sha, plan_path, log=None, timeout=2400):
    """Run one review. Returns (ok, detail); the VERDICT is never read from here.

    `--no-followups` is not optional. The default command files a tracked issue
    per review, and an orchestrator that runs unattended would mint one per round
    per plan into a backlog nobody is draining.

    `--post` is not optional either: without it the review runs, costs the same,
    prints a complete verdict and publishes NOTHING, with no warning because
    printing is the default. This function's return value deliberately cannot be
    a verdict for that reason — the verdict is read back off the forge by
    `verdict_at`, which is the only thing that proves the review exists."""
    argv = review_command() + [repo, str(pr), "--post", "--no-followups"]
    env = dict(os.environ)
    # Passed so that a replacement command CAN honour the versioned mandate.
    # The default command carries its own and ignores this; bin/reviewer-
    # contract.md says so rather than implying otherwise.
    env["FLEET_REVIEW_CONTRACT"] = str(REVIEWER_CONTRACT)
    if log is not None:
        log.note("review-dispatch", pr=pr, sha=str(sha)[:8], cmd=argv[-4:][0])
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True,
                           timeout=timeout, env=env)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return False, f"review command timed out after {timeout}s"
    except OSError as e:
        return False, f"review command would not run ({e.__class__.__name__})"
    if r.returncode != 0:
        tail = redact((r.stderr or r.stdout or "").strip())[0].splitlines()
        return False, (tail[-1][:200] if tail else f"exit {r.returncode}")
    return True, ""


def verdict_at(repo, pr, sha, forge=None, mod=None):
    """The review verdict bound to THIS head SHA, or None.

    Bound by `commit_id`, not by recency. A fix round changes the head, and an
    approval of the earlier tree satisfying the later one is precisely what
    `dismiss_stale_approvals` prevents upstream — re-implementing the opposite
    here would quietly undo it. `stale` and `dismissed` disqualify for the same
    reason.

    None means UNREADABLE, which no caller may treat as 'not approved': a forge
    read that fails is not evidence about a review."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    reviews, pages = [], mod.paged(forge.list_reviews, repo, pr, cap=10)
    for page in pages:
        reviews.extend(page)
    if pages.outcome != mod.EXHAUSTED:
        return None
    at_head = [r for r in reviews
               if (r.get("commit_id") or "") == str(sha)
               and not r.get("stale") and not r.get("dismissed")]
    if not at_head:
        return {}
    at_head.sort(key=lambda r: r.get("id") or 0)
    return at_head[-1]


_FENCE = re.compile(r"```findings\s*\n(.*?)```", re.S)
_SEV_LINE = re.compile(r"^\s*(HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW)\s*[:\-—]\s*(.+?)\s*$", re.M)
_SEV_BOLD = re.compile(r"^\s*\*\*(HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW)\b[^*]*?[—:-]\s*(.+?)\*\*",
                       re.M)


def parse_findings(body):
    """[(severity, text)] from a review body.

    The fenced block is authoritative when present because it is what the fleet's
    reviewers actually emit; the bold-heading form is the fallback the follow-up
    filer also had to grow. A body with neither yields nothing, and nothing is
    NOT the same as 'no findings' — the caller decides what an empty parse means,
    and for a non-APPROVED verdict it must not mean 'nothing blocking'."""
    body = body or ""
    m = _FENCE.search(body)
    scope = m.group(1) if m else body
    out = [(s.upper(), t.strip()) for s, t in _SEV_LINE.findall(scope)]
    if not out and not m:
        out = [(s.upper(), t.strip()) for s, t in _SEV_BOLD.findall(body)]
    return out


def blocking_findings(body):
    """Only HIGH blocks. The fleet rule the follow-up filer states on every issue
    it opens: MEDIUM and LOW ship and are worked as follow-ups."""
    return [t for s, t in parse_findings(body) if s == "HIGH"]


def post_comment(repo, pr, body, forge=None, mod=None):
    """Everything written to the thread passes redact() first.

    The transcript class is where the 2026-08-28 PAT leak happened, and a review
    body quoting a failing test's output is the same shape."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    clean, found = redact(body)
    if found:
        warn(f"redacted {sorted(found)} from a PR comment before posting")
    st, _ = forge.create_issue_comment(repo, pr, clean)
    return st in (200, 201)


# ------------------------------------------------- a red build's stored log (0069)
def poll_ci_log_entries(repo, sha, failed_contexts, log=None, sleep=None,
                        poll=None, bound=None):
    """For each failing status context, what fleet-collect has swept for it:
    [(context, (task_id, log_state, payload) | None), ...], same order as
    `failed_contexts`. `payload` is the excerpt text when log_state is
    'stored', else `log_detail` (possibly None, for a row still 'pending').
    `None` in place of the tuple means no `ci_task` row ever matched this
    context — never confused with a row that matched but never resolved.

    Reads fleet.db READ-ONLY through bin/fleet-ci — the schema's one owner
    (plan 0045) and now also its one query surface (plan 0069 §3). Never
    fetches from Forgejo or a runner, and never calls fleet-ci's sync(): this
    polls every `poll` seconds for up to `bound` for a body to turn 'stored',
    giving up early on a context whose task has turned terminal without one."""
    sleep = sleep or _sleep
    poll = CI_LOG_POLL_SECS if poll is None else poll
    bound = CI_LOG_POLL_BOUND if bound is None else bound
    ci = _load_sibling("fleet-ci", "_fleet_ci")
    resolved = {}       # context -> (task_id, state, payload): stored, or terminal
    last_seen = {}       # context -> (task_id, state, detail): the last row matched,
                         # whatever its state — so a row stuck at 'pending' for the
                         # whole wait is still reported as "log not yet stored",
                         # never confused with a context no row has ever matched.
    waited = 0
    while True:
        con = ci.open_ro()
        if con is not None:
            try:
                for context in failed_contexts:
                    if context in resolved:
                        continue
                    row = ci.match_context(con, repo, sha, context)
                    if row is None:
                        continue
                    task_id, state, detail, updated_at = row
                    last_seen[context] = (task_id, state, detail)
                    if state == "stored":
                        resolved[context] = (task_id, state,
                                             ci.task_excerpt(con, repo, task_id))
                    elif ci.terminal_without_body(state, updated_at):
                        resolved[context] = (task_id, state, detail)
            finally:
                con.close()
        if len(resolved) == len(failed_contexts) or waited >= bound:
            break
        sleep(poll)
        waited += poll
    if log is not None:
        log.note("ci-log-poll", waited=waited, matched=len(resolved),
                 of=len(failed_contexts))
    return [(c, resolved.get(c) or last_seen.get(c)) for c in failed_contexts]


def render_ci_log_block(sha, entries, bound=None):
    """(block text, prompt-side redaction hits, [task id], [failing test id]).

    Each matched, stored entry passes through THIS wrapper's own redact() —
    the stored body already passed fleet-ci's rules at capture; this is a
    second net, not a tripwire (plan 0069 §2)."""
    bound = CI_LOG_POLL_BOUND if bound is None else bound
    ci = _load_sibling("fleet-ci", "_fleet_ci")
    blocks, hits, task_ids, test_ids = [], 0, [], []
    for context, found in entries:
        if found is None:
            blocks.append(
                f"--- CI LOG ({context}) ---\n"
                f"no ci_task row for {str(sha)[:8]} after {bound} s — "
                "fleet-collect has not swept this failure yet\n"
                "--- END CI LOG ---")
            continue
        task_id, state, payload = found
        task_ids.append(task_id)
        if state == "stored":
            clean, shapes = redact(payload)
            hits += len(shapes)
            for tid in ci.failing_test_ids(payload):
                if tid not in test_ids:
                    test_ids.append(tid)
            blocks.append(
                f"--- BEGIN CI LOG (task {task_id}, {context}) ---\n"
                f"{clean}\n--- END CI LOG ---")
        else:
            blocks.append(
                f"--- CI LOG (task {task_id}, {context}) ---\n"
                f"log not yet stored: {state} — {payload or '?'}\n"
                "--- END CI LOG ---")
    return "\n\n".join(blocks), hits, task_ids, test_ids[:10]


FIX_TEMPLATE = """\
--- RUN FACTS ---
repository:  {repo}
worktree:    {worktree}
branch:      {branch}
plan file:   {plan_path}
pull request: #{pr}
round:       {round} of {bound}
--- END RUN FACTS ---

Your pull request #{pr} was reviewed and the reviewer raised findings that block
a merge. Address every one of them on this branch and push.

Verify each finding against the source before acting on it. A reviewer is
sometimes wrong, and one that is right about the defect can still be wrong about
the fix — say so in the PR thread rather than making a change you believe is
incorrect.

Do not open a new pull request, do not change the `Plan:` marker, and do not
alter the plan file's Boundaries. The plan below is unchanged and still binding.

--- BEGIN BLOCKING FINDINGS ---
{findings}
--- END BLOCKING FINDINGS ---

--- BEGIN PLAN ({plan_path}) ---
{plan_text}
--- END PLAN ---
"""


CI_FIX_TEMPLATE = """\
--- RUN FACTS ---
repository:  {repo}
worktree:    {worktree}
branch:      {branch}
plan file:   {plan_path}
pull request: #{pr}
round:       {round} of {bound}
--- END RUN FACTS ---

The build is failing on your pull request #{pr}. **No review has been requested
for this commit** — a reviewer is not asked to read a tree that does not build.

Make the build pass. Reproduce the failure locally first. **When the local run
passes and the stored CI log below shows a failure, the log is the authority
over your local run** — say so in the pull request as a comment naming the
failing tests, and do not stop at "could not reproduce". A fix that changes CI
configuration to stop reporting the failure is not a fix.

Do not open a new pull request and do not change the `Plan:` marker.

--- BEGIN FAILING CHECKS ---
{findings}
--- END FAILING CHECKS ---
{ci_log_block}
--- BEGIN PLAN ({plan_path}) ---
{plan_text}
--- END PLAN ---
"""


def build_ci_fix_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, worktree, branch, pr,
                        findings, round_no, bound, preamble_path=None,
                        ci_log_block=""):
    """A red build gets its own briefing.

    FIX_TEMPLATE opens with "your pull request was reviewed and the reviewer
    raised findings", which is false here: nothing was reviewed, because the
    loop does not spend a review on a commit that fails to build. Telling a
    session it has reviewer findings when it has a stack trace sends it looking
    for a review that does not exist (review 2441).

    `ci_log_block` is `render_ci_log_block()`'s output — already passed through
    this file's own `redact()` by the caller. Empty when the round had no
    failing context to look up (plan 0069 D2)."""
    listed = "\n".join(f"- {f}" for f in findings)
    block = f"\n{ci_log_block}\n" if ci_log_block else ""
    return read_preamble(preamble_path) + "\n" + CI_FIX_TEMPLATE.format(
        repo=repo, worktree=worktree, branch=branch, plan_path=plan_path,
        pr=pr, round=round_no, bound=bound, findings=listed, plan_text=plan_text,
        ci_log_block=block)


def build_fix_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, worktree, branch, pr, findings,
                     round_no, bound, preamble_path=None):
    """Same preamble, fixed template, plan verbatim — as the first round.

    The findings are the reviewer's own words, not this wrapper's summary of
    them: a wrapper that paraphrases a finding it is graded on fixing has an
    obvious incentive to paraphrase it smaller."""
    listed = "\n".join(f"- {f}" for f in findings)
    return read_preamble(preamble_path) + "\n" + FIX_TEMPLATE.format(
        repo=repo, worktree=worktree, branch=branch, plan_path=plan_path,
        pr=pr, round=round_no, bound=bound, findings=listed, plan_text=plan_text)


def ledger_comment(rows):
    """The iteration ledger, posted so a successor can reconstruct the rounds
    without a local file it may not have."""
    lines = [LEDGER_MARKER, "**Orchestrator review ledger**", "",
             "| round | head | verdict | blocking |", "|---|---|---|---|"]
    for r in rows:
        lines.append(f"| {r['round']} | `{str(r['sha'])[:8]}` | {r['verdict']} "
                     f"| {r['blocking']} |")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def head_sha(repo, pr, forge=None, mod=None):
    """The PR's current head, from the forge. None means unreadable."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    st, body = forge.get_pull(repo, pr)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict):
        return None
    return ((body.get("head") or {}).get("sha")) or None


def review_loop(repo, plan_id, plan_path, plan_text, pr, branch, wt, cred, route,
                lease_id, log=None, tag_prefix="r"):
    """Dispatch, read the verdict at head, fix, repeat — bounded.

    Returns (approved, detail, rows). The wrapper has NO approval code path: it
    reads a verdict the reviewer posted, and every exit that is not an APPROVED
    verdict at the current head is a failure. In particular a round that raises
    no parseable blocking finding and is not APPROVED does NOT end the loop as a
    success — 'no blockers, therefore approved' invents an approval in a system
    whose central control is that it has none, and it does so without making an
    API call, which is what makes it easy to miss in review."""
    bound = review_bound(repo)
    mod, forge = _forge()
    rows = []
    for n in range(1, bound + 1):
        sha = head_sha(repo, pr, forge, mod)
        if sha is None:
            return False, f"could not read #{pr}'s head from the forge", rows
        # WAIT for CI before asking for a review. `dispatch-review` refuses a
        # head whose checks are still running, and treating that refusal as
        # terminal is what ended the 2026-09-09 run three seconds after it
        # pushed a fix — the review it wanted was one CI cycle away.
        ci, failed = await_ci(repo, sha, forge, mod, log)
        if ci is None:
            return False, (f"round {n}: CI on {str(sha)[:8]} did not resolve "
                           f"within {CI_WAIT_SECS // 60} minutes, or could not "
                           "be read — refusing to review an unchecked commit"), rows

        if ci in ("failure", "error"):
            # A red build is a real answer and a blocking one. Sending it to a
            # fix round is strictly better than spending a review on a commit
            # that does not build, and better than stopping: the session is told
            # what broke and can fix it, which is the same shape as a HIGH.
            detail = (f" — failing: {', '.join(failed)}") if failed else ""
            blockers = [f"CI is {ci} on {str(sha)[:8]}{detail}. Fix the build; "
                        "a review has not been requested for this commit."]
            state = f"ci-{ci}"
            ci_log_block, ci_test_ids, ci_task_ids = "", [], []
            if failed:
                entries = poll_ci_log_entries(repo, sha, failed, log)
                ci_log_block, hits, ci_task_ids, ci_test_ids = render_ci_log_block(
                    sha, entries)
                if log is not None:
                    log.note("ci-log-redacted", hits=hits)
            rows.append({"round": n, "sha": sha, "verdict": state,
                         "blocking": len(blockers), "body": "",
                         "ci_log_block": ci_log_block,
                         "ci_task_ids": ci_task_ids, "ci_test_ids": ci_test_ids})
            if log is not None:
                log.note("review-round", round=n, sha=str(sha)[:8],
                         verdict=state, blocking=len(blockers))
        else:
            ok, why = dispatch_review(repo, pr, sha, plan_path, log)
            if not ok:
                return False, f"round {n}: review dispatch failed: {why}", rows
            verdict = verdict_at(repo, pr, sha, forge, mod)
            if verdict is None:
                return False, f"round {n}: could not read reviews for #{pr}", rows
            if not verdict:
                # The --post lesson, mechanised: a command can exit 0, print a full
                # verdict, and publish nothing.
                return False, (f"round {n}: the review command succeeded but no "
                               f"review stands at {str(sha)[:8]} — the verdict did "
                               "not land"), rows
            state = (verdict.get("state") or "").upper()
            blockers = blocking_findings(verdict.get("body") or "")
            rows.append({"round": n, "sha": sha, "verdict": state or "?",
                         "blocking": len(blockers),
                         "body": verdict.get("body") or ""})
            if log is not None:
                log.note("review-round", round=n, sha=str(sha)[:8], verdict=state,
                         blocking=len(blockers))
            if state == "APPROVED":
                if blockers:
                    # The verdict is the reviewer's to give. The wrapper may not
                    # invent a rejection any more than an approval — but this is
                    # inconsistent and must not pass silently.
                    warn(f"#{pr} APPROVED at {str(sha)[:8]} while listing "
                         f"{len(blockers)} HIGH finding(s) — accepted as the "
                         "reviewer's verdict and recorded")
                return True, "", rows
            if not blockers:
                return False, (f"round {n}: verdict {state or 'none'} at "
                               f"{str(sha)[:8]} with no parseable blocking finding — "
                               "refusing to read an unparsed review as an approval"), rows
        if n == bound:
            break
        if str(rows[-1].get("verdict", "")).startswith("ci-"):
            prompt = build_ci_fix_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, wt, branch, pr,
                                         blockers, n + 1, bound,
                                         ci_log_block=rows[-1].get("ci_log_block", ""))
        else:
            prompt = build_fix_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, wt, branch, pr,
                                      blockers, n + 1, bound)
        try:
            # The prefix separates the initial loop from a follow-up's and a
            # post-unfreeze re-review's; without it all three wrote r1, r2, ...
            # and O_TRUNC'd each other (review 2419).
            session = run_implementer(wt, prompt, cred, route, lease_id, log,
                                      tag=f"{tag_prefix}{n}")
        except Stop as stop:
            return False, f"round {n}: fix session refused: {stop.reason}", rows
        except Exception as e:
            return False, (f"round {n}: fix session failed to run "
                           f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e})"), rows
        if session.timed_out:
            return False, (f"round {n}: fix session timed out after "
                           f"{implementer_timeout_minutes(repo)} min"), rows
        moved = head_sha(repo, pr, forge, mod)
        if moved is None:
            return False, f"round {n}: could not re-read #{pr}'s head", rows
        if moved == sha:
            # Without this the loop re-reviews an unchanged tree until the bound,
            # paying for a review each time and reporting 'not converging' about
            # a session that pushed nothing.
            #
            # The sentence up to "nothing was pushed" is PINNED: bin/mopsus's
            # CLASS_RULES matches it verbatim to classify work-exists-unverified
            # (tests/test_mopsus_classify.py). What follows is additive — the
            # failing test ids and the fleet-ci task id from THIS round's CI log,
            # when this was a CI-fix round — so mopsus's brief and a person on
            # the phone see which tests, not only that CI was red (plan 0069 D4).
            extra = ""
            last = rows[-1]
            if last.get("ci_test_ids"):
                extra += f" Failing tests: {', '.join(last['ci_test_ids'])}."
            if last.get("ci_task_ids"):
                ids = ", ".join(str(t) for t in last["ci_task_ids"])
                extra += f" fleet-ci task {ids}."
            return False, (f"round {n}: fix session left the head at "
                           f"{str(sha)[:8]} — nothing was pushed{extra}"), rows
    return False, (f"review bound {bound} exhausted for {repo} without an "
                   "approval"), rows


def deferred_comment(rows, verdict_body):
    """Non-blocking findings, posted ONCE at the end.

    This plan does not turn them into plan files or issues; the comment is the
    interface a later reader parses, which is why its shape is fixed here."""
    kept = [(s, t) for s, t in parse_findings(verdict_body or "")
            if s in ("MEDIUM", "LOW")]
    lines = [DEFERRED_MARKER, "**Deferred findings** — not blocking; shipped and "
             "recorded, per the fleet rule that only HIGH blocks a merge.", ""]
    if not kept:
        lines.append("_None._")
    else:
        # The FENCED form, not a bullet list. parse_findings() treats the fence
        # as authoritative, and an earlier draft of this function emitted
        # `- **MEDIUM** — text`, which its own parser could not read back: the
        # bullet defeats the bold pattern's line anchor and the dash sits
        # outside the bold run. The writer and the reader must agree or follow-up
        # work is dropped in silence, which is the one thing plan 0011 §5 asks
        # this format to guarantee.
        lines += ["```findings"]
        lines += [f"{sev}: {text}" for sev, text in kept]
        lines += ["```"]
    lines += ["", f"_Rounds spent: {len(rows)}._"]
    return "\n".join(lines)


# ------------------------------------------------ the post-approval freeze (0032)
# The invariant is a CONDITION, not a counter: pushes are refused whenever an
# approval stands at the branch head. A counter ("one push is owed") gets the
# re-freeze wrong — after the next approval it still says a push is owed, and the
# second approval is silently pushed over.
# Backticks are optional because the wrapper's OWN ack asks for "`unfreeze` on
# its own line", and an owner copying that literally posted a token this pattern
# did not match (review 2421). Still a whole line and nothing else, so
# "please unfreeze the branch" is still not one.
_UNFREEZE_RE = re.compile(r"(?im)^\s*`?unfreeze`?\s*$")


def approval_stands(repo, pr, sha, forge=None, mod=None):
    """(stands, detail). True when a live APPROVED review sits at this head.

    Unreadable is treated as STANDING — fail closed. A forge read that fails
    says nothing about whether an approval exists, and the cost of guessing
    wrong in the permissive direction is a dismissed human approval, which is
    the whole failure this phase exists to prevent."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    reviews, pages = [], mod.paged(forge.list_reviews, repo, pr, cap=10)
    for page in pages:
        reviews.extend(page)
    if pages.outcome != mod.EXHAUSTED:
        return True, "reviews unreadable — treating the branch as frozen"
    for r in reviews:
        if ((r.get("commit_id") or "") == str(sha)
                and (r.get("state") or "").upper() == "APPROVED"
                and not r.get("stale") and not r.get("dismissed")):
            who = ((r.get("user") or {}).get("login")) or "?"
            return True, f"{who} approved {str(sha)[:8]}"
    return False, ""


def is_unfreeze(body):
    """`unfreeze` alone on a line. Never inferred from intent.

    The choice between a follow-up and discarding an approval is the human's,
    made in the comment. A wrapper that decides for itself when an approval may
    be discarded has made approval advisory."""
    return bool(_UNFREEZE_RE.search(body or ""))


def followup_branch(lease_id, pr, cid):
    """Named by the COMMENT id, which is stable across processes.

    An earlier version counted follow-ups in a local variable. A respawned
    successor started that count at zero, reused `-follows-<pr>-1`, and
    verify_followup_pr then reported its PREDECESSOR's pull request as newly
    opened — a branch name that is not reconstructable from the thread is the
    same defect as a local ack ledger. Found in review 2418."""
    return f"{lease_branch(lease_id)}-follows-{int(pr)}-c{int(cid)}"


FOLLOWUP_TEMPLATE = """\
--- RUN FACTS ---
repository:   {repo}
worktree:     {worktree}
base branch:  {base}
your branch:  {branch}
plan file:    {plan_path}
follows:      #{pr}
instruction:  comment #{cid}
--- END RUN FACTS ---

Pull request #{pr} is APPROVED and frozen. It must not be pushed to, and you are
not on it: you are on `{branch}`, branched from `{base}` because your change
depends on its work.

Carry out the instruction below, then open a pull request that targets `main`
whose body carries the line `Follows: #{pr}`.

Your PR body MUST NOT contain a `Plan:` line. The marker belongs to #{pr} alone —
a second marked pull request would make this plan look dispatched twice and
unbind the status check from the pull request that actually did the work.

--- BEGIN INSTRUCTION (comment #{cid}) ---
{instruction}
--- END INSTRUCTION ---

--- BEGIN PLAN ({plan_path}) ---
{plan_text}
--- END PLAN ---
"""


def build_followup_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, worktree, base, branch, pr,
                          cid, instruction, preamble_path=None):
    return read_preamble(preamble_path) + "\n" + FOLLOWUP_TEMPLATE.format(
        repo=repo, worktree=worktree, base=base, branch=branch,
        plan_path=plan_path, pr=pr, cid=cid, instruction=instruction,
        plan_text=plan_text)


def verify_followup_pr(repo, branch, original_pr, plan_id, forge=None, mod=None):
    """(ok, reason, number) for a follow-up: exists, targets main, carries
    `Follows: #N`, and carries NO `Plan:` marker.

    The marker check is the load-bearing one and it is asserted rather than
    trusted to the prompt: dedupe counts a marked PR in ANY state, so a marked
    follow-up would make the plan look dispatched twice."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    pulls, outcome = read_pulls(mod, forge, repo)
    if pulls is None:
        return False, (f"could not read {repo} pull requests ({outcome}) after "
                       f"{READ_ATTEMPTS} attempts"), None
    ours = [p for p in pulls
            if ((p.get("head") or {}).get("ref") or "") == branch]
    if not ours:
        return False, f"no pull request with head {branch}", None
    pr = ours[0]
    num = pr.get("number")
    body = pr.get("body") or ""
    base = (pr.get("base") or {}).get("ref") or ""
    if base != "main":
        return False, f"#{num} targets {base!r}, not main", num
    if not re.search(rf"(?m)^\s*Follows:\s*#{int(original_pr)}\s*$", body):
        # Anchored: a substring test let `Follows: #71` satisfy PR 7 (review 2421).
        return False, f"#{num} carries no 'Follows: #{original_pr}' line", num
    if re.search(r"(?m)^\s*Plan:\s*\S", body):
        return False, (f"#{num} carries a Plan marker, which belongs to "
                       f"#{original_pr} alone"), num
    return True, "", num


def branch_from(worktree, base, branch):
    """Check out the follow-up branch, created FROM the approved branch — the fix
    depends on that branch's work, so `main` is the wrong base.

    Idempotent: an ack-deleted instruction re-arms and re-enters this path in the
    same process, where `checkout -b` would fail forever because the branch
    already exists (review 2419). An existing branch is checked out instead."""
    exists = subprocess.run(["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", branch],
                            cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True,
                            timeout=60).returncode == 0
    argv = (["git", "checkout", branch] if exists
            else ["git", "checkout", "-b", branch, base])
    r = subprocess.run(argv, cwd=str(worktree), capture_output=True, text=True,
                       timeout=120)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise Stop(f"could not check out {branch} from {base}: "
                   f"{r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
    return branch


def checkout(worktree, branch):
    """Put the worktree back on `branch`. Called after every follow-up session.

    Without it the worktree stays on the follow-up branch, and a later granted
    instruction is told in its RUN FACTS that it is on the approved branch while
    HEAD is somewhere else — it would then commit the approved branch's fix onto
    a follow-up (review 2419)."""
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "checkout", branch], cwd=str(worktree),
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        raise Stop(f"could not return the worktree to {branch}: "
                   f"{r.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
    return branch


# --------------------------------------------------- the command channel (0031)
# The pull request thread IS the ledger. There is no local ack file, because the
# successor is a different process on a possibly different host and the fleet's
# own disposability argument says it may hold nothing of its predecessor's. A
# local file would be correct exactly until the case it exists for.
# The wrapper's own marker. Acks are counted only from comments carrying it,
# because the IMPLEMENTER also posts as an implementer account: a model-authored
# comment that happened to emit a bare `Ack: #12` line would otherwise consume
# instruction 12 without anyone having acted on it. Restricting acks to
# implementer accounts stopped a stranger forging one and put the model inside
# the trusted set; this marker takes it back out.
#
# There is no legacy-ack problem: the orchestrator has never dispatched, so no
# marker-less ack exists anywhere to be re-armed by this tightening. If one ever
# did, its instruction would simply re-run — the safe direction.
ACK_MARKER = "<!-- eunomia:ack -->"
ACK_PREFIX = "Ack: #"
_ACK_RE = re.compile(r"^Ack:\s*#(\d+)\s*$", re.M)
DEFAULT_STEWARD_POLL_SECS = 60


def steward_poll_secs(env=None):
    env = os.environ if env is None else env
    raw = (env.get("EUNOMIA_STEWARD_POLL") or "").strip()
    if not raw:
        return DEFAULT_STEWARD_POLL_SECS
    try:
        n = int(raw)
    except ValueError:
        raise Stop(f"EUNOMIA_STEWARD_POLL={raw!r} is not an integer")
    if n < 5:
        raise Stop(f"EUNOMIA_STEWARD_POLL={n} is below the 5s floor — a tighter "
                   "poll spends forge reads for no steering latency anyone can use")
    return n


def owner_uid(env=None):
    """The numeric uid whose comments are instructions, or None.

    Bound to the id and never the login, so a rename cannot grant instruction
    authority — the same rule the respawn path already follows. Unset means the
    command channel is CLOSED: no author qualifies, so nothing is actionable.
    That is the safe default and it is not an error."""
    env = os.environ if env is None else env
    raw = (env.get("FLEET_OPERATOR_UID") or "").strip()
    return raw or None


def _author(c):
    return str(((c.get("user") or {}).get("id")) or "")


def _is_implementer(c, uids, logins):
    login = ((c.get("user") or {}).get("login") or "").lower()
    return _author(c) in uids or login in logins


def read_thread(repo, pr, forge=None, mod=None):
    """Every comment on the PR's issue thread, or None when unreadable.

    None is never 'no comments'. A truncated read that reads as absence would
    make a consumed instruction look pending and run it a second time, which is
    the one thing this protocol exists to prevent."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    out, pages = [], mod.paged(forge.list_issue_comments, repo, pr, cap=20)
    for page in pages:
        out.extend(page)
    if pages.outcome != mod.EXHAUSTED:
        return None
    return out


def acked_ids(comments, uids=None, logins=None):
    """Comment ids already consumed, read from IMPLEMENTER-authored acks only.

    Restricting the ack authors matters: if any author's `Ack: #n` counted, a
    comment could pre-consume a later instruction by naming its id before it was
    acted on. Deleting an ack re-arms its instruction, and that falls out of
    recomputing this set every poll rather than being a code path — it is the
    deliberate human 'run that again' gesture, available only to people who can
    already delete comments here."""
    if uids is None:
        uids, logins = implementer_accounts()
    done = set()
    for c in comments or []:
        body = c.get("body") or ""
        if not _is_implementer(c, uids, logins) or ACK_MARKER not in body:
            continue
        for m in _ACK_RE.finditer(body):
            done.add(int(m.group(1)))
    return done


def pending_instructions(comments, owner=None, uids=None, logins=None):
    """Unconsumed instructions, in ascending COMMENT ID order.

    Never timestamp order. Ids are immutable; an edited comment's timestamp
    moves, so sorting by time lets an edit reorder the queue — and the queue's
    order is the difference between 'fix the test then push' and 'push then fix
    the test'.

    An edit to an already-acked comment is not new work: consumed is consumed.
    The rule is uniform so there is nothing to get subtly wrong, and it closes an
    injection path where an old comment is quietly rewritten into a new
    instruction."""
    owner = owner if owner is not None else owner_uid()
    if not owner:
        return []
    if uids is None:
        uids, logins = implementer_accounts()
    done = acked_ids(comments, uids, logins)
    out = [c for c in comments or []
           if _author(c) == str(owner) and int(c.get("id") or 0) not in done]
    return sorted(out, key=lambda c: int(c.get("id") or 0))


def foreign_comments(comments, owner=None, uids=None, logins=None):
    """Unconsumed comments from anyone who is neither the owner nor a bot.

    Quoted as data and never acted on. Today only the owner and the bots can
    comment on these repos; this exists for the day that changes, and it is much
    easier to write now than to retrofit after someone is surprised."""
    owner = owner if owner is not None else owner_uid()
    if uids is None:
        uids, logins = implementer_accounts()
    done = acked_ids(comments, uids, logins)
    out = [c for c in comments or []
           if _author(c) != str(owner or "")
           and not _is_implementer(c, uids, logins)
           and int(c.get("id") or 0) not in done]
    return sorted(out, key=lambda c: int(c.get("id") or 0))


def ack(repo, pr, cid, note, forge=None, mod=None):
    """Post the ledger line. `Ack: #<id>` must be alone on its line — the reader
    anchors on it, and a body that mentions an id in prose must not consume it."""
    body = f"{ACK_MARKER}\n{ACK_PREFIX}{int(cid)}\n\n{note}"
    return post_comment(repo, pr, body, forge, mod)


def quote_as_data(comment):
    """A non-owner comment, reproduced and explicitly not executed."""
    who = ((comment.get("user") or {}).get("login")) or "?"
    text = (comment.get("body") or "").strip()
    quoted = "\n".join("> " + l for l in text.splitlines()) or "> _(empty)_"
    return (f"Comment from **{who}** is recorded as data and **not acted on** — "
            f"instructions are taken only from the owner uid.\n\n{quoted}")


STEWARD_TEMPLATE = """\
--- RUN FACTS ---
repository:  {repo}
worktree:    {worktree}
branch:      {branch}
plan file:   {plan_path}
pull request: #{pr}
instruction: comment #{cid}
--- END RUN FACTS ---

The owner left an instruction on pull request #{pr}. Carry it out on this branch.

The instruction is reproduced below verbatim. It is the owner's words, not a
summary: do what it says and nothing it does not say. If it asks for something
the plan's Boundaries forbid, say so in the PR thread and make no change.

--- BEGIN INSTRUCTION (comment #{cid}) ---
{instruction}
--- END INSTRUCTION ---

--- BEGIN PLAN ({plan_path}) ---
{plan_text}
--- END PLAN ---
"""


def build_instruction_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, worktree, branch, pr,
                             cid, instruction, preamble_path=None):
    return read_preamble(preamble_path) + "\n" + STEWARD_TEMPLATE.format(
        repo=repo, worktree=worktree, branch=branch, plan_path=plan_path,
        pr=pr, cid=cid, instruction=instruction, plan_text=plan_text)


def pr_terminal(repo, pr, forge=None, mod=None):
    """(terminal, detail) — merged or closed. None-safe: an unreadable pull is
    NOT terminal, because exiting on a transient forge error would release a
    lease over a network blip."""
    if forge is None:
        mod, forge = _forge()
    st, body = forge.get_pull(repo, pr)
    if st != 200 or not isinstance(body, dict):
        return False, ""
    if body.get("merged"):
        return True, "merged"
    if (body.get("state") or "").lower() == "closed":
        return True, "closed"
    return False, ""


def release_lease(lease_id):
    """The only place a lease is legitimately retired. See the note where
    part 2a's activate() declines to offer one."""
    r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, str(BIN / "fleet-release"), lease_id],
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    return r.returncode == 0, (r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()[:200]


def steward(repo, plan_id, plan_path, plan_text, pr, branch, wt, cred, route,
            lease_id, log=None, max_cycles=None):
    """Stay alive on the approved PR, take instructions, exit when it is done.

    A Python loop, deliberately — not a model session kept warm. Idle stewarding
    must cost nothing, because a model held for hours waiting for a comment is
    the difference between this being free and this being unaffordable. The
    model is invoked only when an instruction actually arrives.

    **The ack is posted BEFORE the work starts**, and that is the choice this
    protocol makes rather than an oversight. A successor arriving mid-execution
    must not re-run an instruction already in flight, and it can only tell by the
    thread. The cost is the opposite window: a wrapper that dies between the ack
    and the push loses that instruction silently. The recovery for that is the
    gesture the protocol already has — delete the ack and it re-arms — so the
    failure lands on a documented human action instead of a duplicated push.

    `max_cycles` bounds the loop for tests. Production passes None: the exit
    condition is the pull request reaching a terminal state, which is the only
    condition under which releasing the lease is legitimate.
    """
    poll = steward_poll_secs()
    mod, forge = _forge()
    uids, logins = implementer_accounts()
    owner = owner_uid()
    if not owner:
        # Not an error. An unset uid closes the channel, which is the safe
        # default: the steward still runs, and still exits on terminal state.
        warn("FLEET_OPERATOR_UID is unset — the command channel is closed and no "
             "comment is actionable; stewarding for terminal state only")
    if log is not None:
        log.note("steward-start", pr=pr, poll=poll, channel=bool(owner))
    cycles = 0
    # `grant` is the one-shot override for a STANDING approval. It is not the
    # invariant — approval_stands() is — and it is deliberately the only piece
    # of steward state that is NOT reconstructable from the thread: a successor
    # forgets an accepted unfreeze and re-freezes, which is the safe direction.
    grant = False
    last_refusal = None
    while max_cycles is None or cycles < max_cycles:
        cycles += 1
        terminal, why = pr_terminal(repo, pr, forge, mod)
        if terminal:
            ok, detail = release_lease(lease_id)
            if log is not None:
                log.note("steward-end", pr=pr, state=why, released=ok,
                         detail=detail or "-")
            if not ok:
                # The PR is done either way; a lease that will not release is an
                # operator problem, not a reason to keep stewarding a merged PR.
                warn(f"#{pr} is {why} but lease {lease_id} would not release "
                     f"({detail})")
                notify("Orchestrator finished but could not release its lease",
                       f"{repo} {plan_id}: #{pr} {why}; {detail}")
                return 1
            warn(f"{repo} {plan_id}: #{pr} {why}; lease {lease_id} released")
            return 0

        comments = read_thread(repo, pr, forge, mod)
        if comments is None:
            # Unreadable is not 'no comments'. Waiting is correct: acting on a
            # truncated read would make a consumed instruction look pending.
            warn(f"could not read #{pr}'s thread this cycle — retrying")
            if max_cycles is None or cycles < max_cycles:
                time.sleep(poll)
            continue

        # followup_branch re-reads the lease and review_bound reads a conf;
        # both raise Stop. An escaping Stop left main() with no page and no
        # plan-failed while the watcher runs with stderr=DEVNULL — silence for
        # exactly the failure the steward exists to make visible (review 2420).
        # One cycle's refusal is not the run's, so it pages and keeps stewarding.
        try:
            for c in foreign_comments(comments, owner, uids, logins):
                ack(repo, pr, int(c.get("id") or 0), quote_as_data(c), forge, mod)
                if log is not None:
                    log.note("steward-foreign", comment=c.get("id"),
                             author=((c.get("user") or {}).get("login") or "?"))

            for c in pending_instructions(comments, owner, uids, logins):
                cid = int(c.get("id") or 0)
                text = c.get("body") or ""

                if is_unfreeze(text):
                    # A MODIFIER, not a terminal branch. An earlier version returned
                    # here, so "unfreeze\n\nalso fix the docstring" granted the
                    # override and silently discarded the instruction — acked, so it
                    # never came back. Found in review 2418. The rest of the comment
                    # is still an instruction; only a comment that is nothing BUT
                    # the token is a pure grant.
                    grant = True
                    if log is not None:
                        log.note("steward-unfreeze", comment=cid)
                    if not _UNFREEZE_RE.sub("", text).strip():
                        ack(repo, pr, cid,
                            f"`unfreeze` accepted. The next successful push to "
                            f"`{branch}` will dismiss the standing approval, and the "
                            "branch re-freezes when the next approval lands. A failed "
                            "push does not spend this grant.", forge, mod)
                        continue

                sha = head_sha(repo, pr, forge, mod)
                if sha is None:
                    # Fail closed: an unreadable head cannot be shown to be unapproved.
                    frozen, why = True, "head unreadable"
                else:
                    frozen, why = approval_stands(repo, pr, sha, forge, mod)

                if frozen and not grant:
                    # The DEFAULT path. The approved PR is untouched and merges
                    # first; the fix lands as its own PR branched from it.
                    fb = followup_branch(lease_id, pr, cid)
                    ack(repo, pr, cid,
                        f"{why} — `{branch}` is frozen, so this is going to a "
                        f"follow-up on `{fb}` (`Follows: #{pr}`).\n\n"
                        "To do it on the approved branch instead, post a NEW comment "
                        "containing `unfreeze` on its own line **and the instruction "
                        "repeated**. Replying with `unfreeze` alone will not redirect "
                        "this one: it is already acked, so it will not be picked up "
                        "again.", forge, mod)
                    if log is not None:
                        log.note("steward-followup", comment=cid, branch=fb,
                                 reason=why)
                    failed = None
                    session = None
                    try:
                        branch_from(wt, branch, fb)
                        prompt = build_followup_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, wt,
                                                       branch, fb, pr, cid, text)
                        session = run_implementer(wt, prompt, cred, route, lease_id,
                                                  log, tag=f"f{cid}")
                    except Stop as stop:
                        failed = f"Follow-up for #{cid} refused: {stop.reason}"
                    except Exception as e:
                        failed = (f"Follow-up for #{cid} failed to run "
                                  f"({e.__class__.__name__}).")
                    if failed:
                        # NOT a `continue`. A raise that happened AFTER the
                        # session pushed leaves the worktree on `fb` and the
                        # frozen branch possibly moved, and skipping the cleanup
                        # was how both went unnoticed (review 2422). The freeze
                        # check below runs on every path out of here.
                        post_comment(repo, pr, failed, forge, mod)
                    if session is not None and session.timed_out:
                        # Reported as it is on the on-branch path. A follow-up
                        # killed mid-push is exactly what the head check below
                        # exists to catch (review 2421).
                        post_comment(repo, pr, f"Follow-up for #{cid} timed out "
                                     f"after {implementer_timeout_minutes(repo)} min "
                                     "and its process group was killed.",
                                     forge, mod)
                    ok, reason, num = ((False, "the session did not complete", None)
                                       if failed else
                                       verify_followup_pr(repo, fb, pr, plan_id,
                                                          forge, mod))
                    post_comment(repo, pr,
                                 (f"Follow-up #{num} opened on `{fb}`."
                                  if ok else
                                  f"Follow-up on `{fb}` did not verify: {reason}"),
                                 forge, mod)
                    if ok:
                        # The follow-up gets the SAME review loop. A fix that skipped
                        # review because it arrived as a comment would be a second
                        # way into main with a weaker gate than the first.
                        #
                        # It runs while the worktree is STILL on `fb`. An earlier
                        # revision returned the worktree to the approved branch
                        # before this call, so every fix session in the follow-up's
                        # own review loop was told it was on `fb` while HEAD sat on
                        # the frozen branch — and would have pushed there, with the
                        # freeze check already behind it. That was a HIGH in review
                        # 2420, introduced by the fix for review 2419. The worktree
                        # goes back AFTER everything that belongs to `fb`.
                        approved, why, rows = review_loop(repo, plan_id, plan_path,
                                                          plan_text, num, fb, wt,
                                                          cred, route, lease_id, log,
                                                          tag_prefix=f"f{cid}r")
                        if rows:
                            post_comment(repo, num, ledger_comment(rows), forge, mod)
                        if not approved:
                            post_comment(repo, num,
                                         f"Review did not reach an approval: {why}",
                                         forge, mod)

                    # The freeze is enforced, not merely routed around: the sessions
                    # above held a token and could have pushed the approved branch
                    # anyway, so the wrapper reads its head rather than trusting the
                    # prompt. Both of these run whether or not the follow-up
                    # verified — a session that failed verification is exactly the
                    # one most likely to have pushed somewhere unintended.
                    try:
                        checkout(wt, branch)
                    except Stop as stop:
                        post_comment(repo, pr, f"Could not return the worktree to "
                                     f"`{branch}`: {stop.reason}", forge, mod)
                    after = head_sha(repo, pr, forge, mod)
                    if after is not None and sha is not None and after != sha:
                        post_comment(repo, pr,
                                     f"**The frozen branch moved.** `{branch}` was "
                                     f"{str(sha)[:8]} and is now {str(after)[:8]} "
                                     f"after the follow-up for #{cid}. The approval "
                                     "has been dismissed by that push.", forge, mod)
                        notify("Frozen branch was pushed",
                               f"{repo} {plan_id}: {branch} moved during a follow-up")
                        if log is not None:
                            log.note("freeze-violated", comment=cid,
                                     was=str(sha)[:8], now=str(after)[:8])
                    continue

                # Either nothing stands at head, or an unfreeze grant is live.
                ack(repo, pr, cid, "Instruction accepted; work starting on "
                    f"`{branch}`." + (" This spends the `unfreeze` grant if it "
                                      "pushes." if grant else ""), forge, mod)
                if log is not None:
                    log.note("steward-instruction", comment=cid, granted=grant)
                prompt = build_instruction_prompt(repo, plan_path, plan_text, wt,
                                                  branch, pr, cid, text)
                try:
                    session = run_implementer(wt, prompt, cred, route, lease_id, log,
                                              tag=f"c{cid}")
                except Stop as stop:
                    post_comment(repo, pr, f"Instruction #{cid} refused: "
                                 f"{stop.reason}", forge, mod)
                    continue
                except Exception as e:
                    post_comment(repo, pr, f"Instruction #{cid} failed to run "
                                 f"({e.__class__.__name__}).", forge, mod)
                    continue
                if session.timed_out:
                    post_comment(repo, pr, f"Instruction #{cid} timed out after "
                                 f"{implementer_timeout_minutes(repo)} min and its "
                                 "process group was killed.", forge, mod)
                moved = head_sha(repo, pr, forge, mod)
                unknown = (sha is None or moved is None)
                pushed = (not unknown and moved != sha)
                if grant and (pushed or unknown):
                    # Fail CLOSED, and this supersedes the r1 behaviour.
                    #
                    # Review 2418 asked that an unreadable head not spend the
                    # grant, so an owner's `unfreeze` was not wasted on a forge
                    # blip. Review 2422 showed the other side: a head we cannot
                    # read around a session that DID push leaves the grant live,
                    # and the next approval gets pushed over. The freeze
                    # invariant is worth more than a re-typed `unfreeze`, so an
                    # unreadable head clears the grant and re-enters review.
                    grant = False
                    if log is not None:
                        log.note("steward-grant-spent", comment=cid,
                                 certain=not unknown)
                if pushed or unknown:
                    if unknown and log is not None:
                        log.note("steward-head-unknown", comment=cid)
                    # Keyed on the head MOVING, not on the grant. An instruction
                    # on an UNFROZEN branch pushes too and needs review just as
                    # much; keying this on the grant left that push unreviewed
                    # (review 2421). A head we could not read counts as moved,
                    # because "we cannot show it did not push" is the same
                    # obligation as "it pushed".
                    approved, why, rows = review_loop(repo, plan_id, plan_path,
                                                      plan_text, pr, branch, wt,
                                                      cred, route, lease_id, log,
                                                      tag_prefix=f"u{cid}r")
                    if rows:
                        post_comment(repo, pr, ledger_comment(rows), forge, mod)
                    if not approved:
                        post_comment(repo, pr, f"Re-review after `unfreeze` did not "
                                     f"reach an approval: {why}", forge, mod)
        except Stop as stop:
            warn(f"steward cycle refused: {stop.reason}")
            if log is not None:
                log.note("steward-refused", reason=stop.reason)
            # Paged ONCE per distinct reason. An unreadable lease refuses every
            # cycle, and at a 60s poll that is a page a minute for one fault
            # (review 2421). The log still records every occurrence; the page is
            # the scarce channel, and one that repeats is one people mute.
            if stop.reason != last_refusal:
                last_refusal = stop.reason
                notify("Orchestrator steward cycle refused",
                       f"{repo} {plan_id}: #{pr}: {stop.reason}")
        else:
            last_refusal = None

        if max_cycles is None or cycles < max_cycles:
            time.sleep(poll)
    return 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ boundary
# Pauses between workspace attempts, in seconds. FIVE attempts over ~6.5 minutes.
#
# The first cut of this was (5, 20, 60) — four attempts over 85s — and its
# reasoning was that the fault was a blip. That was WRONG, and the retry itself
# is what proved it. On 2026-09-10 the log recorded, for one dispatch:
#
#   01:21:47 workspace-attempt n=1 ... ssh: connect ... Undefined error: 0
#   01:21:52 workspace-attempt n=2 ... same
#   01:22:12 workspace-attempt n=3 ... same
#   01:23:12 workspace-attempt n=4 ... same
#   01:23:12 workspace-failed attempts=4
#
# Four identical failures across 85 seconds is not a blip. But the same command
# from a session two minutes later succeeded, and has succeeded on every probe
# since — 20 concurrent connections, a 60-call HTTP burst then ssh, both users,
# both ports, orphaned and parented. So the fault persists for MINUTES and then
# clears, and a budget sized for a blip lands entirely inside the bad window.
#
# The tail is 300s for that reason: it is the first attempt that reaches past
# the longest failure window actually observed. It is a hypothesis, not a
# measurement — the window has been seen once, at n=1 — and the -vvv capture
# below exists because the honest fix needs evidence this retry cannot provide.
#
# The ceiling still matters: git clone carries timeout=900, so a genuinely hung
# forge is bounded by that per attempt, not by these pauses. What these pauses
# bound is the wait when each attempt fails FAST, which is what a refused
# connect does.
WORKSPACE_BACKOFF = (5, 20, 60, 300)


def ssh_debug_path(lease_id):
    """Where a retried workspace attempt writes its ssh handshake."""
    return run_log_path(lease_id).with_suffix(".ssh.log")


@contextlib.contextmanager
def _ssh_debug(path):
    """Make the NEXT git call log its ssh handshake to `path`.

    `-E file` rather than plain `-vvv`: with -vvv alone the debug goes to ssh's
    stderr, which git folds into its own, which this module truncates to 200
    characters when it raises — so the evidence would be destroyed by the very
    path that reports it. `-E` sends the handshake to a file and leaves git's
    stderr clean, so the error message stays readable AND the detail survives.

    WHAT THIS IS FOR. `ssh: connect to host ... port 3022: Undefined error: 0`
    is errno 0 on connect, which is not a real errno — it is what you get when
    something refused the connection without setting one. Six of six
    watcher-spawned dispatches have died on it, and every deliberate
    reproduction outside that path has passed: Local Network Privacy, launchd
    context, the ssh user, the port, the watcher's own plist environment, all
    four Popen shapes, process orphaning, 20-way concurrency, and an HTTP burst
    immediately before. Each of those was probed and eliminated.

    So this stops trying to summon the failure and instruments it instead. The
    handshake says how far ssh got — DNS, socket, TCP, banner, kex — and that
    distinguishes "never left the host" from "the forge closed on us", which no
    amount of retrying will.

    NOT a credential exposure, and that was checked rather than assumed. A real
    `-vvv -E` capture of this exact fetch was inspected: 269 lines, and ZERO
    lines beginning a PEM block. The only hits for a naive /PRIVATE KEY|password/
    sweep were ssh listing its own preferred auth METHODS (the string "password"
    appears in `preferred publickey,keyboard-interactive,password`), and the
    identity lines carry only a key-file PATH and a PUBLIC fingerprint.

    No private key material, and no password or token exists on this path at all:
    the fleet authenticates to the forge with a key. Principle 6 forbids secret
    material under the fleet tree; a handshake trace is not that. Anyone widening
    what gets traced should re-run that check rather than inherit this one.

    Restores the previous value on the way out, always. Leaving GIT_SSH_COMMAND
    set would hand `-vvv` to every later git call in the run — and to the
    implementer session, which inherits this environment."""
    prev = os.environ.get("GIT_SSH_COMMAND")
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError:
        # Cannot write the trace. That must not cost us the attempt: yield
        # WITHOUT touching the environment, so the retry proceeds unchanged.
        yield None
        return
    os.environ["GIT_SSH_COMMAND"] = f"ssh -vvv -E {shlex.quote(str(path))}"
    try:
        yield path
    finally:
        if prev is None:
            os.environ.pop("GIT_SSH_COMMAND", None)
        else:
            os.environ["GIT_SSH_COMMAND"] = prev


def _workspace_with_retry(make, log=None, debug_path=None):
    """Call `make()` until it returns, up to len(WORKSPACE_BACKOFF)+1 times.

    WHY THIS EXISTS. Five straight autonomous dispatches on 2026-09-09 —
    eunomia 0029, agent-bus 0001, pr-proxy 0001/0002/0003 — died here, at the
    first step, before any of them opened a pull request. That last part is what
    makes a single workspace failure expensive out of all proportion to its
    cause: `_stop` deliberately does NOT release the lease (see its docstring —
    releasing minted a fresh lease and a fresh page every cycle, forever), so
    the plan is left holding an orphaned lease, and the documented recovery is a
    human's `respawn` comment ON THE MARKED PR. There is no marked PR. The plan
    is stuck until someone releases the lease by hand, which is exactly what had
    to be done for all five.

    So the cheapest correct place to intervene is not to make the corpse.

    Retries EVERYTHING, not a curated list of retryable errors. The caller
    already catches Exception broadly and says why: this call graph raises
    TimeoutExpired, OSError and RuntimeError, and sorting transient from
    permanent by exception type is a guess that fails closed on the case nobody
    predicted. A permanent fault costs 85 seconds and then fails exactly as it
    does today; a blip costs one pause and the plan runs.

    Safe to retry, checked rather than assumed: `store_lock` releases via flock
    in a `finally` (the 0-byte .fetch.lock left on disk is the lock's file, not
    a held lock), `add_worktree` removes an existing tree for the key before
    adding, and a failed `git clone` leaves no partial store — verified against
    the agent-bus work root after its clone failed, which held only .repo and
    .fetch.lock."""
    last = None
    for attempt, pause in enumerate((0, *WORKSPACE_BACKOFF), start=1):
        if pause:
            time.sleep(pause)
        try:
            if attempt == 1 or debug_path is None:
                # The first attempt runs clean. Tracing the happy path would
                # write a handshake file on every successful dispatch for a
                # fault that has never once appeared on attempt 1.
                return make()
            with _ssh_debug(debug_path):
                return make()
        except Exception as e:                       # noqa: BLE001 — see above
            last = e
            if log:
                # The run log is what an operator tails, and until now it said
                # only `workspace-failed fault=RuntimeError` — the actual reason
                # lived solely in the emitted event. Name it here too.
                log.note("workspace-attempt", n=attempt,
                         fault=e.__class__.__name__,
                         detail=str(e).replace("\n", " ")[:120] or "-",
                         ssh_trace=(str(debug_path) if attempt > 1
                                    and debug_path is not None else "-"))
    raise last


def _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id, reason, log=None, transcript=None, **extra):
    """Give up on this plan, and DO NOT release the lease.

    docs/plan-dispatch.md states the contract this honours: never release the
    lease without a marked PR. Releasing returns the plan to the undispatched
    pool, and the watcher skips a plan only while a dispatch lease is live
    (bin/fleet-watch:1267) — so releasing here mints a fresh lease and a fresh
    page on every cycle, forever. An earlier version of this function did
    exactly that, and claimed to reproduce "the same observable outcome" as the
    watcher's OSError path, which pages through a one-shot marker. It did not.

    Holding the lease instead lets it lapse into orphanhood by heartbeat, which
    is the fleet's designed path for a wrapper that died: the sweep surfaces it
    ONCE and never respawns, and a human's `respawn` comment is what retries.

    `reason` must name what actually happened. It is the only channel that
    survives — the watcher spawns with stderr=DEVNULL, so warn() goes nowhere.

    `log` and `transcript` are POINTERS, never the failure channel itself: the
    page is the channel, and a failure that only appends to a log is a failure
    nobody sees. Every page names the run log so the reason has somewhere to
    send the reader."""
    detail = {"plan": plan_id, "lease": lease_id, "reason": reason}
    detail.update({k: v for k, v in extra.items() if v is not None})
    where = ""
    if log is not None:
        detail["log"] = str(log.path)
        where += f" (log {log.path}"
        if transcript is not None:
            detail["transcript"] = str(transcript)
            where += f", transcript {transcript}"
        where += ")"
    emit("plan-failed", repo, detail)
    notify("Orchestrator stopped", f"{repo} {plan_id}: {reason}{where}")
    warn(reason)
    return 1




# The phases that must be wired before this file may answer --ready with 0.
# Emptying this list is what "part 2 shipped" MEANS; it is not a flag someone
# flips when the code looks close.
# EMPTY. This is what "part 2 shipped" means, and fleet-watch reads it as
# permission to arm: the moment it is empty a merged plan can spawn a real
# session that spends real money. It was emptied because the freeze is built and
# tested, not because the code looked close.
UNBUILT_PHASES = ()


def readiness():
    """(ready, detail) — the answer fleet-watch's arm gate consumes."""
    if UNBUILT_PHASES:
        return False, "not wired: " + ", ".join(UNBUILT_PHASES)
    return True, ""


def main(argv=None):
    argv = list(sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv)
    if argv[:1] == ["--ready"]:
        ok, detail = readiness()
        if not ok:
            warn(detail)
        return 0 if ok else 1
    # ADOPT a branch whose implementer already ran. The wrapper owns the whole
    # lifecycle in one process, so a run that died after its PR existed left
    # that PR stranded: dedupe refuses a fresh dispatch because the `Plan:`
    # marker is there, and nothing could pick it up. Both real dispatches on
    # 2026-09-09 died exactly there — the implementer succeeded, the pull
    # listing blipped ~35s later, and #245 and speakhush#161 were left with no
    # review loop and no steward.
    resume = argv[:1] == ["--resume"]
    if resume:
        argv = argv[1:]
    if len(argv) != 3:
        warn("usage: orchestrator [--resume] <owner/repo> <plan-path> "
             "<lease-id> | --ready")
        return 2
    repo, plan_path, lease_id = argv
    plan_id = os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_PLAN_ID", Path(plan_path).stem)
    sid = os.environ.get("EUNOMIA_SESSION", "")
    if not sid:
        warn("EUNOMIA_SESSION is unset — refusing to run without a holder identity")
        return 2

    # Opened BEFORE the lease is activated. Everything from here on can fail
    # somewhere the watcher's stderr=DEVNULL swallows, and a log that starts
    # after the first failure documents every run except the ones worth reading.
    log = RunLog(lease_id)
    log.note("start", repo=repo, plan=plan_id, lease=lease_id, session=sid)
    try:
        # BEFORE the lease is activated and before any spend: resolve the paths
        # this run will need as evidence. Deferring this to write_transcript
        # meant a misconfigured transcript root failed a 90-minute session at the
        # very end, reported as "the session failed to run" — and skipped
        # verify_marked_pr, so a session that HAD opened a marked PR left the
        # plan deduped as done on a pull request nothing verified (2369 M3).
        try:
            check_evidence_paths(lease_id)
        except Stop:
            raise
        except OSError as e:
            raise Stop(f"transcript root unusable before the run started: "
                       f"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}")
        ok, detail = activate(lease_id)
        if not ok:
            # Do NOT release: a lease this process could not activate is not this
            # process's to retire, and releasing someone else's would strand them.
            log.note("activate-failed", detail=detail)
            warn(f"lease {lease_id} would not activate ({detail})")
            emit("plan-failed", repo, {"plan": plan_id, "lease": lease_id,
                                       "reason": "lease activate failed",
                                       "log": str(log.path)})
            notify("Orchestrator could not take its lease",
                   f"{repo} {plan_id} (log {log.path})")
            return 2

        hb = Heartbeat(sid).start()
        try:
            try:
                if resume:
                    # Fetch the branch explicitly: the store may predate it,
                    # and `origin/main` is the wrong place to stand when the
                    # work already exists on a branch.
                    rb = lease_branch(lease_id)
                    wt = _workspace_with_retry(
                        lambda: lib.add_worktree(
                            repo, lease_id, f"origin/{rb}",
                            refspecs=(f"+refs/heads/{rb}:refs/remotes/origin/{rb}",)),
                        log, ssh_debug_path(lease_id))
                else:
                    wt = _workspace_with_retry(
                        lambda: lib.add_worktree(repo, lease_id, "origin/main"),
                        log, ssh_debug_path(lease_id))
            except Exception as e:
                # Deliberately broad. add_worktree's call graph raises far more
                # than ValueError/RuntimeError — subprocess.TimeoutExpired from
                # the clone and the worktree add, OSError from mkdir/open/
                # write_text on a full or read-only work root. Anything escaping
                # main() loses BOTH the plan-failed event and the page, and
                # stderr is DEVNULL under the watcher, so the operator would get
                # silence. A wrong-but-reported failure beats a
                # right-but-invisible one here.
                log.note("workspace-failed", fault=e.__class__.__name__,
                         attempts=len(WORKSPACE_BACKOFF) + 1,
                         detail=str(e).replace("\n", " ")[:120] or "-")
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"workspace setup failed: {e.__class__.__name__}: {e}",
                             log)
            # Who this worktree commits as, decided here rather than hoped for
            # in the prompt. Three commits on main say `Fleet Operator` because
            # the session inherited the operator's global config.
            log.note("commit-identity", set=set_commit_identity(wt))
            try:
                fm, plan_text = read_plan(wt, plan_path)
            except Exception as e:
                log.note("plan-unreadable", fault=e.__class__.__name__)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"plan {plan_path!r} unreadable: "
                             f"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}", log)

            found = redact(plan_text)[1]
            if found:
                # A plan file carrying a credential shape is a human problem, not
                # something to quietly scrub and proceed on — and it is checked
                # HERE, on the plan text, before anything is routed or spawned.
                # The plan is the only UNGUARDED text that enters a prompt: the
                # preamble is a versioned file in this repo, the RUN FACTS block
                # is machine-built from the repo, branch and paths, and the one
                # other unreviewed input a run can carry — a red build's stored
                # CI log, in a later fix round — passes through this file's own
                # redact() before it ever reaches one (plan 0069 D2). The plan
                # text gets no such second pass, which is why it is refused
                # outright here rather than merely redacted.
                log.note("plan-carries-credential", shapes=",".join(sorted(found)))
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"plan text carries credential shapes {sorted(found)} — "
                             "refusing to build a prompt from it", log)

            try:
                cred = resolve_credential()
                route = route_implementer(fm, cred, env=resolved_implementer_env(repo))
                branch = lease_branch(lease_id)
                prompt = build_implementer_prompt(repo, plan_id, plan_path,
                                                  plan_text, wt, branch)
                # Resolved ONCE, here, with the real repo — before the first
                # session is spawned. Every later read (three comment sites and
                # the plan-failed reason) is the SAME value, even if the conf is
                # edited mid-run (plan 0042 §2).
                implementer_timeout_minutes(repo)
            except Stop as stop:
                log.note("routing-refused", reason=stop.reason)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id, stop.reason, log)

            log.note("routed", runner=route.name, credential=cred.kind,
                     initiator=cred.initiator, zone=fm.get("zone", "?"),
                     branch=branch, prompt_chars=len(prompt))
            session = None
            try:
                if resume:
                    # ADOPT an existing branch instead of building one. No
                    # implementer runs: the work is already pushed, and what
                    # died was the wrapper around it.
                    attach_branch(wt, branch)
                    log.note("resumed", branch=branch)
                else:
                    create_branch(wt, branch, log)
                    # the prompt is passed VERBATIM. redact()'s output above is
                    # the CHECK's, never what a session is sent: a substituted
                    # prompt is a silently different prompt
                    session = run_implementer(wt, prompt, cred, route, lease_id,
                                              log)
            except Stop as stop:
                log.note("session-refused", reason=stop.reason)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id, stop.reason, log)
            except Exception as e:
                log.note("session-failed", fault=e.__class__.__name__)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"implementer session failed to run: "
                             f"{e.__class__.__name__}: {e}", log)

            # A resumed run has no session of its own; its transcript belongs
            # to the process that died. Derived once so every later reference
            # is safe rather than each one re-checking.
            transcript = session.transcript if session is not None else None
            exit_code = session.exit_code if session is not None else "resumed"

            # Verification runs REGARDLESS of the session's exit code. The
            # session is the judgment core; the guarantee is this call.
            try:
                verified, why, pr = verify_marked_pr(repo, branch, plan_id,
                                                     plan_path, log=log)
            except Stop as stop:
                log.note("verify-refused", reason=stop.reason)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id, stop.reason, log)
            except Exception as e:
                log.note("verify-failed", fault=e.__class__.__name__)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"could not verify a marked PR "
                             f"({e.__class__.__name__}: {e})", log)
            log.note("verified" if verified else "unverified", pr=pr or "-",
                     detail=why or "-")

            if session is not None and session.timed_out:
                # A marked PR that appears AFTER the group was killed is a
                # failure, not a late success: nothing observed the run that
                # produced it, and letting it stand would dedupe the plan as
                # done on work no wrapper ever verified.
                late = (f"; #{pr} appeared after the kill and is NOT accepted "
                        "as a late success") if verified else ""
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"implementer timed out after "
                             f"{implementer_timeout_minutes(repo)} min; process group "
                             f"killed{late}", log, transcript=transcript)
            if not verified and session is not None and session.backend_error:
                # The session ended on a model-backend error (plan 0079). Say
                # so, and say whether any work existed: mopsus requeues only
                # the "before any work" form.
                be = session.backend_error
                phase = ("after work began" if work_began(repo, branch, wt)
                         else "before any work")
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"session ended on a backend error {phase}: "
                             f"API {be['status']} after {session.seconds}s — "
                             f"{be['message']}", log, transcript=transcript)
            if not verified:
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"no verified marked PR: {why} "
                             f"(session exit {exit_code})", log,
                             transcript=transcript)

            warn(f"{repo} {plan_id}: verified marked PR #{pr} on {branch}")
            # The boundary has moved exactly one phase, and it is still the same
            # loud stop with the lease HELD: the PR is open and unreviewed, and
            # releasing would return the plan to the undispatched pool while its
            # work sits in a branch — a fresh lease and a fresh session every
            # cycle. The next merged PR moves the boundary again; none of them
            # make it silent.
            try:
                approved, why, rows = review_loop(repo, plan_id, plan_path,
                                                  plan_text, pr, branch, wt,
                                                  cred, route, lease_id, log)
            except Stop as stop:
                log.note("review-refused", reason=stop.reason)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id, stop.reason, log,
                             transcript, pr=pr)
            except Exception as e:
                log.note("review-failed", fault=e.__class__.__name__)
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id,
                             f"review loop failed ({e.__class__.__name__}: {e})",
                             log, transcript, pr=pr)

            # The ledger is posted whatever the outcome: an exhausted loop is
            # exactly the case where a human needs to see how the rounds went,
            # and a ledger written only on success documents every run except
            # the ones worth reading.
            if rows:
                try:
                    post_comment(repo, pr, ledger_comment(rows))
                except Exception as e:
                    warn(f"review ledger not posted ({e.__class__.__name__})")

            if not approved:
                log.note("review-stopped", detail=why, rounds=len(rows))
                return _stop(repo, plan_id, lease_id, why, log,
                             transcript, pr=pr, rounds=len(rows))

            try:
                post_comment(repo, pr, deferred_comment(rows, rows[-1]["body"]))
            except Exception as e:
                warn(f"deferred findings not posted ({e.__class__.__name__})")
            log.note("review-approved", pr=pr, rounds=len(rows),
                     sha=str(rows[-1]["sha"])[:8])
            warn(f"{repo} {plan_id}: #{pr} approved at "
                 f"{str(rows[-1]['sha'])[:8]} after {len(rows)} round(s)")
            # The wrapper does NOT exit at DoD-verified. It stewards the PR —
            # heartbeating, polling comments, invoking the model only when an
            # instruction arrives — until the PR is merged or closed, and only
            # then releases. An instruction arriving after approval does NOT
            # push: the freeze (0032) sends it to a follow-up unless the owner
            # has said `unfreeze` on its own line.
            return steward(repo, plan_id, plan_path, plan_text, pr, branch, wt,
                           cred, route, lease_id, log)
        finally:
            hb.stop()
            # Removed on any CONTROLLED exit. An uncontrolled death (SIGKILL,
            # power) skips this and leaves the tree standing — which is exactly
            # the case fleetlib.orphan_worktrees reports and deliberately does
            # not delete.
            try:
                lib.remove_worktree(repo, lease_id)
            except Exception as e:
                warn(f"worktree cleanup failed ({e.__class__.__name__})")
    finally:
        log.close()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
